1
00:00:02,544 --> 00:00:05,255
ANNOUNCER : The following commentary is by Sir Christopher Frayling ,

2
00:00:05,339 --> 00:00:08,548
film historian , expert on horror literature and film ,

3
00:00:08,634 --> 00:00:11,626
and rector of the Royal College of Art in London .

4
00:00:11,720 --> 00:00:13,631
FRAYLING : We begin with a prologue

5
00:00:13,722 --> 00:00:15,383
with actor Edward Van Sloan

6
00:00:15,474 --> 00:00:18,557
coming in front of the curtain and doing a partly teasing ,

7
00:00:18,644 --> 00:00:21,762
partly titillating , partly warning to the audience

8
00:00:21,855 --> 00:00:23,523
that what they 're about to see is a horror movie .

9
00:00:23,607 --> 00:00:27,066
This was in fact added after the previews of Frankenstein

10
00:00:27,236 --> 00:00:29,936
because the studio weren 't quite sure how to handle it .

11
00:00:30,155 --> 00:00:34,365
The prologue was partly written by the young John Huston and others ,

12
00:00:34,451 --> 00:00:36,369
and we don 't actually know who shot it .

13
00:00:36,453 --> 00:00:38,285
It probably wasn 't James Whale .

14
00:00:38,830 --> 00:00:41,117
Edward Van Sloan , veteran actor ,

15
00:00:41,208 --> 00:00:45,076
50 years old , had appeared in at least 150 plays on Broadway ,

16
00:00:45,337 --> 00:00:49,376
and had most recently appeared as Van Helsing in Universal 's Dracula .

17
00:00:49,466 --> 00:00:51,048
The sort of authority figure .

18
00:00:51,134 --> 00:00:54,843
Later , after appearing as Waldman in Frankenstein ,

19
00:00:54,930 --> 00:00:56,806
he would appear as Muller in The Mummy .

20
00:00:56,890 --> 00:01:01,009
So , he became the sort of authority figure in a whole cycle of Universal films .

21
00:01:01,144 --> 00:01:04,230
But actually , the character he plays in Frankenstein is rather different

22
00:01:04,314 --> 00:01:06,681
to the one he played in Dracula .

23
00:01:06,775 --> 00:01:11,144
We begin the credits with a face with rays coming out of the eyes ,

24
00:01:11,238 --> 00:01:14,822
just like the way in which Bela Lugosi had been filmed in Dracula ,

25
00:01:14,992 --> 00:01:19,281
and just as the prologue begins where the end of Dracula finished off ,

26
00:01:19,454 --> 00:01:22,040
with Edward Van Sloan saying , " There are such things , "

27
00:01:22,124 --> 00:01:24,616
so the credits pick up on the visuals of Dracula .

28
00:01:24,835 --> 00:01:27,953
And then we get these eyes in a circular motion .

29
00:01:28,046 --> 00:01:29,923
Again , partly a reference to Bela Lugosi ,

30
00:01:30,007 --> 00:01:32,307
reminding us of the previous Universal product .

31
00:01:32,676 --> 00:01:37,341
But also a reference to a famous scene in Fritz Lang 's Metropolis , 1926 ,

32
00:01:37,806 --> 00:01:41,549
the nightclub scene where the false Maria is doing an erotic dance ,

33
00:01:41,685 --> 00:01:43,311
and the eyes circulate in this way .

34
00:01:43,395 --> 00:01:46,888
So , already we 've got a reference to German expressionist films .

35
00:01:47,316 --> 00:01:50,650
The credits show the rather convoluted history of this production .

36
00:01:50,736 --> 00:01:53,524
It isn 't so much based on the novel by Mary Shelley ,

37
00:01:53,614 --> 00:01:56,777
or Mrs. Percy B. Shelley , as they call her on the credits .

38
00:01:56,867 --> 00:02:00,360
It 's more based on a series of plays , adaptations of Frankenstein ,

39
00:02:00,454 --> 00:02:02,741
which had been happening since 1823 .

40
00:02:02,914 --> 00:02:03,164
Most recently , one by Peggy Webling ,

41
00:02:03,248 --> 00:02:05,205
Most recently , one by Peggy Webling ,

42
00:02:05,292 --> 00:02:08,705
adapted by the American journalist John L. Balderston .

43
00:02:09,087 --> 00:02:10,838
The first scene , the graveyard scene ,

44
00:02:10,922 --> 00:02:14,916
originally had shots of mourners on the horizon carrying a coffin

45
00:02:15,010 --> 00:02:17,126
and bells and flags and banners and so on .

46
00:02:17,220 --> 00:02:19,211
This was cut out and later reused

47
00:02:19,389 --> 00:02:21,933
in the opening sequence of The Bride of Frankenstein .

48
00:02:22,017 --> 00:02:25,726
Instead , we begin with a diagonal shot of a rope and a coffin ,

49
00:02:25,937 --> 00:02:30,898
and we panned across the mourners . We have a cross at a crazy angle .

50
00:02:31,109 --> 00:02:36,024
In fact , on the shooting script , it says , " Crosses are tottering at crazy angles ,

51
00:02:36,114 --> 00:02:40,326
" and we are somewhere in central Europe . " There 's a figure of death in a monk 's cowl .

52
00:02:40,410 --> 00:02:44,904
And just like the credits , we begin with eyes and hands coming down behind it .

53
00:02:45,123 --> 00:02:47,660
As Fritz , played by Dwight Frye ,

54
00:02:48,210 --> 00:02:51,623
who played the Renfield figure , the man who eats insects in Dracula ,

55
00:02:51,713 --> 00:02:52,797
pops up with his pop-eyes .

56
00:02:52,881 --> 00:02:55,842
He 's the man who had a stage whisper that could be heard three miles away .

57
00:02:55,926 --> 00:02:58,964
And behind him , Colin Clive as Frankenstein .

58
00:02:59,805 --> 00:03:04,049
The priest takes away the bell inside its sacramental cover ,

59
00:03:04,518 --> 00:03:07,729
and , were it a Roman Catholic funeral somewhere in central Europe ...

60
00:03:07,813 --> 00:03:09,474
We don 't know what period we are .

61
00:03:09,564 --> 00:03:14,855
As the shooting script said , there 's an angry sky , it 's parched , there 's leafless trees .

62
00:03:15,028 --> 00:03:17,572
And everything 's on the diagonal . Nothing 's vertical .

63
00:03:17,656 --> 00:03:20,523
This is a tottering world that 's about to fall over .

64
00:03:20,617 --> 00:03:22,824
And we now concentrate on the gravedigger .

65
00:03:23,537 --> 00:03:28,282
The microphone was actually placed inside the coffin in order to get the echo effect

66
00:03:28,375 --> 00:03:32,790
as the earth hits the casket , which rather shocked audiences in 1931 .

67
00:03:32,879 --> 00:03:37,419
Remember that horror films hadn 't really caught on yet as a genre of entertainment ,

68
00:03:37,634 --> 00:03:38,843
so it came as quite a surprise .

69
00:03:38,927 --> 00:03:42,716
This is much more physical horror , much more realistic horror

70
00:03:42,806 --> 00:03:45,218
than had been represented in Dracula .

71
00:03:45,434 --> 00:03:47,834
This moment where the gravedigger lights his pipe

72
00:03:47,936 --> 00:03:49,395
isn 't , in fact , in the shooting script .

73
00:03:49,479 --> 00:03:52,187
It was added at the last minute by James Whale ,

74
00:03:52,315 --> 00:03:55,808
and is the first of many references in this film to fire .

75
00:03:56,445 --> 00:03:59,528
The lighting of the match , the flaming torches ,

76
00:03:59,656 --> 00:04:02,273
the mill that burns down at the end ,

77
00:04:02,367 --> 00:04:05,867
the fire from the heavens that gives the spark of life to the creature .

78
00:04:06,329 --> 00:04:09,832
And , in fact , the original novel was called Frankenstein : or , the Modern Prometheus ,

79
00:04:09,916 --> 00:04:14,376
and the whole myth of Prometheus is Prometheus brought down fire from the gods

80
00:04:14,504 --> 00:04:16,923
and tried to bring it onto Earth and was punished for it ,

81
00:04:17,007 --> 00:04:20,500
just like Frankenstein being punished for challenging the gods

82
00:04:20,677 --> 00:04:24,170
in this piece of spare-part surgery that he engages in .

83
00:04:25,015 --> 00:04:26,722
[ CHURCH BELL TOLLS ]

84
00:04:28,769 --> 00:04:30,937
The bell in the background is also interesting .

85
00:04:31,021 --> 00:04:33,513
It tolls mournfully for the funeral .

86
00:04:33,690 --> 00:04:37,024
Later on , the Angelus will sing , there will be wedding bells ,

87
00:04:37,110 --> 00:04:41,069
and the bell will toll mournfully at the end , as the mill goes up .

88
00:04:41,239 --> 00:04:45,403
So , the sound design of the film was actually remarkably sophisticated for 1931 .

89
00:04:45,869 --> 00:04:48,782
And we 're in for a more visual experience than Dracula was .

90
00:04:48,872 --> 00:04:50,873
Although we 're in the studio at Universal ,

91
00:04:50,957 --> 00:04:54,370
it owes a lot to German expressionist films of the ' 20s .

92
00:04:54,878 --> 00:04:58,041
In fact , James Whale viewed The Cabinet of Dr. Caligari

93
00:04:58,173 --> 00:05:01,416
when preparing Frankenstein , as reported in Variety .

94
00:05:01,510 --> 00:05:06,255
The shooting script makes explicit reference to Metropolis in the laboratory scene .

95
00:05:06,640 --> 00:05:10,059
And there 's no doubt that the creature owes a great deal to The Golem ,

96
00:05:10,143 --> 00:05:11,850
the 1920 German film .

97
00:05:12,187 --> 00:05:13,552
[ CLATTERING ]

98
00:05:17,400 --> 00:05:21,654
The coffin being brought out of the grave isn 't in fact mentioned in the shooting script ,

99
00:05:21,738 --> 00:05:24,776
and again , was added at the last minute .

100
00:05:26,493 --> 00:05:28,609
Now , we 're not sure what period we 're in .

101
00:05:28,703 --> 00:05:32,196
Throughout the film , there 's a strange elision of periods .

102
00:05:32,415 --> 00:05:36,079
Yes , the women 's costumes are 1930 , 1931 .

103
00:05:36,461 --> 00:05:40,011
Yes , the equipment looks very modern . But look , here 's a public hanging .

104
00:05:40,757 --> 00:05:42,925
A body swinging on a gibbet up in the hills .

105
00:05:43,009 --> 00:05:46,798
So , a sort of medieval or pre-18th century reference .

106
00:05:47,264 --> 00:05:51,349
And throughout , you get this funny , dreamlike mixing it with historical periods .

107
00:05:51,434 --> 00:05:55,428
The angry mob is medieval , the laboratory , the watchtower is Gothic ,

108
00:05:55,730 --> 00:05:57,471
sort of 18th , 19th century .

109
00:05:57,607 --> 00:06:00,359
The swinging corpse . And yet , a lot of modern things as well .

110
00:06:00,443 --> 00:06:03,029
And although this is a film that deals with modern science ,

111
00:06:03,113 --> 00:06:05,865
where are the radios ? Where are the cars ? Where are the telephones ?

112
00:06:05,949 --> 00:06:09,035
Where are all the latest developments of science ? They 're not there at all .

113
00:06:09,119 --> 00:06:13,158
We 're in a kind of never-never land that 's partly medieval , partly fairytale ,

114
00:06:13,248 --> 00:06:14,659
and partly modern .

115
00:06:14,875 --> 00:06:18,163
As James Whale said , " It 's all part of the ritual . "

116
00:06:18,295 --> 00:06:20,838
This is an attempt to tell a fairy tale that 's timeless .

117
00:06:20,922 --> 00:06:22,822
And we don 't know where we are either .

118
00:06:23,008 --> 00:06:25,718
It says in the shooting script somewhere in central Europe .

119
00:06:25,802 --> 00:06:28,544
In Peggy Webling 's play , we 're in Switzerland ,

120
00:06:28,638 --> 00:06:30,094
Goldstadt in Switzerland .

121
00:06:30,473 --> 00:06:34,057
In the novel , we 're in France and Switzerland and Germany ,

122
00:06:34,269 --> 00:06:36,813
but it isn 't clear . It 's a kind of never-never land again ,

123
00:06:36,897 --> 00:06:38,747
which is somewhere in central Europe .

124
00:06:42,861 --> 00:06:46,570
But here to remind us we 're in Goldstadt , " Goldstadt Medical College . "

125
00:06:46,656 --> 00:06:48,488
And a lecture theater .

126
00:06:48,700 --> 00:06:52,659
Originally , in the shooting script , the cut was , in fact , to a domestic scene

127
00:06:52,746 --> 00:06:56,865
involving Elizabeth , Frankenstein 's fiancée , and their friend , Victor .

128
00:06:57,167 --> 00:07:00,660
But the order of the scenes was changed to bring in the university .

129
00:07:01,212 --> 00:07:03,203
And here 's Dr. Waldman in action .

130
00:07:03,298 --> 00:07:07,542
And the students not paying any attention . All this laughter and low comedy

131
00:07:07,636 --> 00:07:09,752
was added after the shooting script .

132
00:07:09,930 --> 00:07:12,843
And James Whale added lots of comedic moments to this film

133
00:07:13,016 --> 00:07:17,260
in the lightweight scenes that punctuate the more dramatic Gothic scenes

134
00:07:17,354 --> 00:07:18,435
throughout the film .

135
00:07:18,521 --> 00:07:21,183
He heightened the comedy and he heightened the horror .

136
00:07:21,274 --> 00:07:23,109
And we 're first introduced to these two brains .

137
00:07:23,193 --> 00:07:26,527
The normal brain and the dysfunctio cerebri ,

138
00:07:26,655 --> 00:07:27,941
the abnormal brain ,

139
00:07:28,365 --> 00:07:31,778
which is nothing to do with the novel , nothing to do with the plays .

140
00:07:31,868 --> 00:07:35,611
It was added on the original outline by Robert Florey ,

141
00:07:35,705 --> 00:07:39,323
the French-American director who was originally slotted for Frankenstein .

142
00:07:39,542 --> 00:07:42,628
And he had this idea that you 'd have a good brain and a bad brain ,

143
00:07:42,712 --> 00:07:46,205
and the bad brain would be sewn into the hapless creature 's cranium .

144
00:07:46,716 --> 00:07:49,583
This , of course , altered the entire point of the story

145
00:07:49,678 --> 00:07:51,715
by making it a kind of eugenic thing .

146
00:07:51,805 --> 00:07:54,640
You know , some brains are bad , some people are criminals by birth

147
00:07:54,724 --> 00:07:56,180
for eugenic reasons .

148
00:07:56,267 --> 00:07:59,476
Whereas in the story , it 's all about social responsibility .

149
00:07:59,562 --> 00:08:02,896
The creature becomes ugly because he 's treated as ugly .

150
00:08:03,066 --> 00:08:06,402
And if society rejects people and treats them as seriously disabled ,

151
00:08:06,486 --> 00:08:09,444
then they 're liable to have a rather violent reaction ,

152
00:08:09,531 --> 00:08:10,656
is the theme of the novel .

153
00:08:10,740 --> 00:08:13,618
But in this , it 's all a matter of good brain and bad brain .

154
00:08:13,702 --> 00:08:17,912
It cuts out about half a volume of story in just one tiny sequence .

155
00:08:29,592 --> 00:08:32,929
And here we have Fritz , the vertically challenged laboratory assistant ,

156
00:08:33,013 --> 00:08:38,429
rather like the puffer figure in alchemy , who stands doing the actual craftwork

157
00:08:38,685 --> 00:08:41,052
white the scientist stands in the foreground .

158
00:08:41,229 --> 00:08:45,024
Fritz , a character who goes right back to the original dramatization of Frankenstein

159
00:08:45,108 --> 00:08:50,694
in 1823 , providing someone for Frankenstein to talk to during the laboratory scenes .

160
00:08:51,197 --> 00:08:52,858
He 's taking the brain .

161
00:08:52,949 --> 00:08:56,327
The bouncing skeleton in the background isn 't mentioned in the shooting script ,

162
00:08:56,411 --> 00:08:59,904
another comedy moment , a sort of vaudeville moment , as Fritz ...

163
00:09:00,123 --> 00:09:01,791
Actually , there 's a continuity error there .

164
00:09:01,875 --> 00:09:04,001
It 's bouncing in one of the shots and not in the others .

165
00:09:04,085 --> 00:09:05,503
But Fritz bumps into the skeleton .

166
00:09:05,587 --> 00:09:10,332
So , this rather tense scene of pinching the wrong brain

167
00:09:10,467 --> 00:09:14,301
is also punctuated by comedy , a very James Whale shift of mood .

168
00:09:14,387 --> 00:09:16,799
James Whale loved extreme shifts of mood ,

169
00:09:16,890 --> 00:09:20,383
from comedy to tragedy , from gruesomeness to laughter .

170
00:09:20,643 --> 00:09:23,010
Comedies of manners , of eccentricity .

171
00:09:23,313 --> 00:09:27,227
And so , in the next scene , we have a domestic scene ,

172
00:09:27,692 --> 00:09:30,569
a lightweight scene to follow the nastiness of the laboratory .

173
00:09:30,653 --> 00:09:32,653
And it begins with a series of close-ups

174
00:09:32,781 --> 00:09:34,615
that aren 't again mentioned in the shooting script .

175
00:09:34,699 --> 00:09:37,702
But Whale thought this was the most economic way of presenting

176
00:09:37,786 --> 00:09:41,871
the domestic complexities of the relationships between these characters .

177
00:09:41,998 --> 00:09:44,750
Close-up of the photo of Frankenstein , close-up of the maid ,

178
00:09:44,834 --> 00:09:49,920
close-up of Elizabeth , played by Mae Clarke , close-up of Victor , played by John Boles .

179
00:09:50,090 --> 00:09:52,422
And there 's something between these people .

180
00:09:52,550 --> 00:09:54,760
Frankenstein is engaged to be married to Elizabeth ,

181
00:09:54,844 --> 00:09:56,595
but Victor is a close friend of Elizabeth .

182
00:09:56,679 --> 00:09:59,137
In fact , in the John Balderston adaptation ,

183
00:09:59,224 --> 00:10:03,058
he 's the ex-fiancé of Elizabeth who has been jilted for Frankenstein .

184
00:10:03,686 --> 00:10:06,731
All of that 's done in very quick close-ups at the beginning of the scene

185
00:10:06,815 --> 00:10:08,772
prior to a long dialogue scene .

186
00:10:09,234 --> 00:10:13,273
Mae Clarke had appeared in James Whale 's previous film , Waterloo Bridge ,

187
00:10:13,404 --> 00:10:15,315
as the prostitute , Myra .

188
00:10:15,782 --> 00:10:19,243
And she was having a hell of a year in 1931 , because 1931 was also the year

189
00:10:19,327 --> 00:10:22,121
when she had a grapefruit pushed in her face by James Cagney

190
00:10:22,205 --> 00:10:23,536
in Public Enemy .

191
00:10:23,998 --> 00:10:29,368
She was , in fact , the original of the character Lorelei Lee , Mae Clarke was ,

192
00:10:29,462 --> 00:10:32,045
in Gentlemen Prefer Blondes by Anita Loos ,

193
00:10:32,132 --> 00:10:35,625
because in the 1920s , the mid - '20s , she was a song-and-dance girl

194
00:10:35,885 --> 00:10:39,844
in vaudeville and nightclubs . And the Lorelei Lee character was based upon her .

195
00:10:40,056 --> 00:10:41,797
James Whale cast her in this .

196
00:10:41,891 --> 00:10:44,560
She was slightly apprehensive that she was playing an English character ,

197
00:10:44,644 --> 00:10:46,729
or someone who should speak Shakespearean English , Elizabeth ,

198
00:10:46,813 --> 00:10:48,929
But Whale said , " Don 't worry about that . "

199
00:10:49,274 --> 00:10:54,610
But he cast her because she had an intensity and a kind of shrillness in her performance .

200
00:10:54,904 --> 00:10:58,568
And , as he said , she believed so wholeheartedly in this project ,

201
00:10:58,741 --> 00:11:02,453
she wasn 't going to send it up , that he cast her in preference to Bette Davis ,

202
00:11:02,537 --> 00:11:06,121
who was the actress who was first mentioned with reference to this part .

203
00:11:06,875 --> 00:11:09,958
John Boles was a singer as well as an actor ,

204
00:11:10,044 --> 00:11:13,162
a baritone who appeared in numerous musicals at this time .

205
00:11:13,423 --> 00:11:16,757
And he represents stolid support as Victor .

206
00:11:17,010 --> 00:11:20,262
In Peggy Webling 's play , the names of the characters were juggled around

207
00:11:20,346 --> 00:11:23,759
from the original Mary Shelley novel in all sorts of strange ways .

208
00:11:23,892 --> 00:11:27,351
Victor Frankenstein becomes Henry Frankenstein .

209
00:11:27,437 --> 00:11:29,428
Victor is given to the friend ,

210
00:11:29,856 --> 00:11:34,271
Moritz , which was the surname of the servant in the novel Frankenstein ,

211
00:11:34,402 --> 00:11:36,439
is given as the surname to Victor .

212
00:11:36,779 --> 00:11:39,487
And Henry , which was the name of Henry Clerval ,

213
00:11:39,616 --> 00:11:42,716
Frankenstein 's closest friend , who is murdered by the creature ,

214
00:11:42,869 --> 00:11:44,328
the name is given to Frankenstein .

215
00:11:44,412 --> 00:11:46,914
So Frankenstein becomes Henry , the friend becomes Victor ,

216
00:11:46,998 --> 00:11:48,739
Elizabeth was always Elizabeth ,

217
00:11:48,833 --> 00:11:51,951
except that she 's called Amelia in the John Balderston version ,

218
00:11:52,045 --> 00:11:53,035
if you follow me .

219
00:11:53,129 --> 00:11:54,922
But I don 't know why the names were changed .

220
00:11:55,006 --> 00:11:57,550
Victor is such a good name for the overreaching scientist ,

221
00:11:57,634 --> 00:12:00,592
but in this film , he becomes Henry Frankenstein .

222
00:12:01,054 --> 00:12:02,636
I 'll be ready in a minute .

223
00:12:07,185 --> 00:12:08,435
Herr Frankenstein is a most ...

224
00:12:08,519 --> 00:12:11,063
FRAYLING : And James Whale wanted to build up the Mae Clarke character ,

225
00:12:11,147 --> 00:12:14,139
so he added her to this scene where they visit Dr. Waldman

226
00:12:14,317 --> 00:12:16,558
in his office / laboratory .

227
00:12:16,819 --> 00:12:21,484
Strange mixture again of medieval-modern . Look at the skulls on the shelf behind him ,

228
00:12:21,616 --> 00:12:23,242
and the test tubes in the foreground .

229
00:12:23,326 --> 00:12:27,329
It 's like a medieval image of an alchemist in the background , and in the foreground ,

230
00:12:27,413 --> 00:12:28,706
some images of modern science .

231
00:12:28,790 --> 00:12:32,283
But Elizabeth is there expressing her worries about her fiancé .

232
00:12:32,627 --> 00:12:36,120
And Victor is giving his usual solid support .

233
00:12:37,131 --> 00:12:39,042
Herr Frankenstein is greatly changed .

234
00:12:39,133 --> 00:12:43,127
FRAYLING : Waldman comes over not as a sympathetic Van Helsing character ,

235
00:12:43,221 --> 00:12:47,431
but right from the beginning , as a rather dogmatic elder statesman

236
00:12:47,517 --> 00:12:48,601
who rushes to judgment .

237
00:12:48,685 --> 00:12:51,268
And throughout this film , he tends to say ,

238
00:12:51,354 --> 00:12:54,064
" Kill the monster ! " and " It 's going to be a danger to society . "

239
00:12:54,148 --> 00:12:55,183
" Destroy ! Destroy ! "

240
00:12:55,316 --> 00:12:58,194
And he has a very extreme view . He 's not the voice of reason .

241
00:12:58,278 --> 00:13:00,112
He doesn 't believe that truth lies in the middle .

242
00:13:00,196 --> 00:13:02,437
He tends to adopt rather extreme positions .

243
00:13:02,532 --> 00:13:07,151
And Waldman introduces , in this sequence , the first sustained reference

244
00:13:07,287 --> 00:13:10,370
to the mythological world that this film belongs to ,

245
00:13:10,456 --> 00:13:14,415
linking it to older scenarios of forbidden knowledge .

246
00:13:14,502 --> 00:13:16,128
Going back to the Garden of Eden ,

247
00:13:16,212 --> 00:13:19,204
or the classical myths of Prometheus , Daedalus and Icarus .

248
00:13:19,382 --> 00:13:21,632
If you overreach , you 're going to get burned .

249
00:13:21,718 --> 00:13:23,968
If you deal with knowledge that you shouldn 't ,

250
00:13:24,095 --> 00:13:25,137
you 're going to get into trouble .

251
00:13:25,221 --> 00:13:29,071
In fact , the original novel Frankenstein wasn 't too concerned with this theme ,

252
00:13:29,225 --> 00:13:32,092
this Faustian theme , this theme of overreaching .

253
00:13:32,645 --> 00:13:33,938
It had lots of other themes .

254
00:13:34,022 --> 00:13:36,889
The personal responsibility of the artist and scientist ,

255
00:13:37,233 --> 00:13:39,520
The social responsibility of the scientist .

256
00:13:39,610 --> 00:13:42,068
The acquisition of language through the creature

257
00:13:42,155 --> 00:13:44,066
and educational theories .

258
00:13:44,157 --> 00:13:49,448
Social radicalism and radical social theory coming from her father , William Godwin .

259
00:13:49,579 --> 00:13:52,331
It had all these themes , and indeed , themes about contemporary science .

260
00:13:52,415 --> 00:13:54,622
But the Faustian theme was just one of many .

261
00:13:54,751 --> 00:13:58,710
Through Waldman , it becomes foregrounded as the main point of the story .

262
00:13:59,005 --> 00:14:02,748
And it really sets the tone for mad scientist films from then on .

263
00:14:02,967 --> 00:14:07,131
They 're Faustian films . Nature , good . Science can be bad .

264
00:14:07,430 --> 00:14:09,556
And you mustn 't overreach . There are limits to the kind of knowledge that 's permissible ,

265
00:14:09,640 --> 00:14:11,308
And you mustn 't overreach . There are limits to the kind of knowledge that 's permissible ,

266
00:14:11,392 --> 00:14:14,134
and you 're somehow challenging God if you go further .

267
00:14:14,270 --> 00:14:17,170
In fact , God is scarcely mentioned in Mary Shelley 's novel .

268
00:14:17,273 --> 00:14:21,187
It 's a kind of Romantic , slightly godless universe of overreachers .

269
00:14:21,361 --> 00:14:24,274
The overreaching takes place in this world , not the next .

270
00:14:24,530 --> 00:14:26,567
But there are many references to God ,

271
00:14:26,699 --> 00:14:29,691
including a famous one in this sequence , during the film .

272
00:14:29,786 --> 00:14:31,948
- Fritz ! - Hello .

273
00:14:32,038 --> 00:14:33,369
[ RATTLING ]

274
00:14:33,456 --> 00:14:35,948
Have you finished making those connections ?

275
00:14:36,042 --> 00:14:37,498
Yes , they 're done .

276
00:14:37,627 --> 00:14:39,670
Well , come down then and help with these ...

277
00:14:39,754 --> 00:14:42,504
FRAYLING : Now , the laboratory has a fascinating history .

278
00:14:42,632 --> 00:14:45,590
In the original novel , Mary Shelley simply wrote ,

279
00:14:45,676 --> 00:14:48,134
" I gathered the instruments of life around me . "

280
00:14:48,304 --> 00:14:50,806
That 's all she said about the equipment in the laboratory .

281
00:14:50,890 --> 00:14:54,474
She wasn 't interested in the literalness of how the operation happened .

282
00:14:54,560 --> 00:14:57,177
It was more a mythic fairytale moment .

283
00:14:57,271 --> 00:15:02,061
Subsequently , she talked about the workings of some great engine in 1831 ,

284
00:15:02,151 --> 00:15:04,984
when she reintroduced the novel in its cut-down version .

285
00:15:05,571 --> 00:15:10,782
In the play of 1823 , there 's lots of sparks and flames , but they happen offstage ,

286
00:15:10,993 --> 00:15:14,538
and Fritz tells the audience what 's going on , and suddenly the creature staggers in

287
00:15:14,622 --> 00:15:16,829
through the doors of the laboratory .

288
00:15:16,916 --> 00:15:20,955
In film , in 1910 , the Edison version of Frankenstein

289
00:15:21,170 --> 00:15:23,422
made the laboratory into an alchemist laboratory .

290
00:15:23,506 --> 00:15:26,383
You had a bubbling cauldron , and out of the cauldron comes the creature .

291
00:15:26,467 --> 00:15:29,004
And indeed , in the John Balderston adaptation ,

292
00:15:29,095 --> 00:15:33,805
from which the film was taken , it 's a mixture of sparks and electricity ,

293
00:15:34,016 --> 00:15:38,726
and also the elixir of life , a liquid that 's poured into the creature 's mouth .

294
00:15:39,063 --> 00:15:41,521
But for this film , it 's all modern science .

295
00:15:41,607 --> 00:15:44,109
And James Whale reckoned the entire credibility of the film

296
00:15:44,193 --> 00:15:45,604
depended on this sequence .

297
00:15:45,695 --> 00:15:48,027
If the audience could believe that the creature

298
00:15:48,114 --> 00:15:52,278
was actually having the spark of life , the vitalism of life , put into him ,

299
00:15:52,368 --> 00:15:53,660
then the story would hold together .

300
00:15:53,744 --> 00:15:56,455
If they didn 't believe that , they would simply laugh and say ,

301
00:15:56,539 --> 00:15:59,372
" This is too farfetched , I can 't even watch this film . "

302
00:15:59,584 --> 00:16:02,451
So , the creation sequence was absolutely crucial .

303
00:16:02,545 --> 00:16:03,587
Where did he get it from ?

304
00:16:03,671 --> 00:16:06,538
Well , partly , he got it from Metropolis in 1926 ,

305
00:16:06,632 --> 00:16:10,091
where there 's a long laboratory sequence where the scientist ,

306
00:16:10,219 --> 00:16:12,961
Dr. Rotwang , played by Rudolf Klein-Rogge ,

307
00:16:13,139 --> 00:16:17,508
dressed in a medieval jerkin in a Gothic house in the middle of a modern city

308
00:16:17,602 --> 00:16:21,220
creates an artificial Maria , an artificial robot ,

309
00:16:21,564 --> 00:16:24,556
by transferring essence of real Maria into robot Maria .

310
00:16:24,692 --> 00:16:26,610
And there 's lots of bubbling glassware .

311
00:16:26,694 --> 00:16:29,686
Lots of electric sparks and arcs which are visible .

312
00:16:29,822 --> 00:16:32,780
No sound effects , of course , it was a silent film .

313
00:16:32,909 --> 00:16:36,412
And in fact , the person responsible for the special effects in Metropolis said ,

314
00:16:36,496 --> 00:16:39,289
" The great challenge is how do you visualize electricity ? "

315
00:16:39,373 --> 00:16:42,035
Electricity is invisible . How do you visualize it ?

316
00:16:42,126 --> 00:16:44,169
You 've got to have sparks , you 've got to have arcs .

317
00:16:44,253 --> 00:16:46,870
You 've got to make the audience see electricity .

318
00:16:47,006 --> 00:16:51,250
So , they contacted the electrical engineer and inventor Kenneth Strickfaden ,

319
00:16:51,511 --> 00:16:54,924
who put together the equipment for this laboratory .

320
00:16:55,097 --> 00:16:58,434
It 's all sorts of things . He said any junk that he happened to have around

321
00:16:58,518 --> 00:17:00,350
was put into the styling of it .

322
00:17:00,520 --> 00:17:01,687
And he used all sorts of phrase .

323
00:17:01,771 --> 00:17:03,897
There 's a baritone generator , whatever that is .

324
00:17:03,981 --> 00:17:08,191
There 's a vacuum electrolysis and there 's a nucleus analyzer .

325
00:17:08,277 --> 00:17:11,613
None of these words really mean anything , it 's a kind of mumbo jumbo .

326
00:17:11,697 --> 00:17:14,109
And actually , if you look at the shooting script ,

327
00:17:14,200 --> 00:17:17,784
it has a very impressionistic view of what should be in the laboratory .

328
00:17:17,870 --> 00:17:21,363
A glittering , mysterious apparatus with generators , transformers ,

329
00:17:21,499 --> 00:17:25,083
wave-chargers , diffusers , a large rotary spark gap , et cetera .

330
00:17:25,211 --> 00:17:26,701
Very impressive-looking .

331
00:17:26,796 --> 00:17:28,881
It 's more impressionistic than scientific ,

332
00:17:28,965 --> 00:17:31,923
designed to create a feeling of modern scientific magic .

333
00:17:32,009 --> 00:17:34,797
Something suggestive of the laboratory in Metropolis .

334
00:17:34,887 --> 00:17:36,048
[ POUNDING ON DOOR ]

335
00:17:36,138 --> 00:17:37,128
Not much .

336
00:17:37,223 --> 00:17:39,475
I 'll show them , messing about at this time of night .

337
00:17:39,559 --> 00:17:41,015
Got too much to do .

338
00:17:42,144 --> 00:17:44,556
Wait a minute ! All right ! All right !

339
00:17:44,855 --> 00:17:45,939
FRAYLING : Fritz goes down the stairs ,

340
00:17:46,023 --> 00:17:49,401
and a lot of business is added by James Whale after the shooting script .

341
00:17:49,485 --> 00:17:52,819
A lot of comedy after the intensity of the laboratory scene .

342
00:17:53,030 --> 00:17:56,819
This huge staircase , which owes a lot in fact to The Golem of 1920 ,

343
00:17:56,951 --> 00:17:59,113
and Fritz , stumbling around in the night .

344
00:17:59,203 --> 00:18:02,706
And here we have a very typical James Whale bit of business . As he goes up the steps ,

345
00:18:02,790 --> 00:18:05,873
he pulls up his sock , and then continues up the steps .

346
00:18:06,002 --> 00:18:07,753
Again , that might have been improvised ,

347
00:18:07,837 --> 00:18:11,171
it was certainly added at the last minute in August , 1931 .

348
00:18:11,841 --> 00:18:13,002
Henry !

349
00:18:14,677 --> 00:18:16,167
ELIZABETH : Open the door !

350
00:18:16,262 --> 00:18:17,888
FRAYLING : We 're in the watchtower on the hill .

351
00:18:17,972 --> 00:18:19,872
In the original Robert Florey version ,

352
00:18:20,016 --> 00:18:24,260
it was the mill , where the film finishes , that the laboratory was located in .

353
00:18:24,437 --> 00:18:28,726
In Mary Shelley 's novel , it 's the upstairs room of Frankenstein 's house .

354
00:18:28,816 --> 00:18:29,316
But here it 's a watchtower on a hill .

355
00:18:29,400 --> 00:18:30,734
But here it 's a watchtower on a hill .

356
00:18:30,818 --> 00:18:35,278
Remember , the word Frankenstein means " the rock " or " stone of the Franks . "

357
00:18:35,531 --> 00:18:37,032
They are the people of the mountains ,

358
00:18:37,116 --> 00:18:39,159
the people who live amongst the rocks and stones .

359
00:18:39,243 --> 00:18:42,120
And of course , it has to be a high place , the highest you can find ,

360
00:18:42,204 --> 00:18:45,242
in order to harness the vitalism of the heavens .

361
00:18:50,087 --> 00:18:53,830
The design of the sets by art director Charles D. Hall ,

362
00:18:53,924 --> 00:18:56,256
with Herman Rosse doing the drawings ,

363
00:18:56,344 --> 00:19:00,679
and James Whale , who himself was a trained scene designer and had been to art school ,

364
00:19:00,765 --> 00:19:04,224
supervising it , is one of the most memorable aspects of this film .

365
00:19:04,852 --> 00:19:09,562
From German expressionism in the ' 20s , where everything is on the diagonal ,

366
00:19:09,690 --> 00:19:13,558
where you have painted sets , where the madness of the sets

367
00:19:13,653 --> 00:19:17,146
is an outward and a visible sign of the madness of the characters .

368
00:19:17,281 --> 00:19:21,946
It moves on , in this film , into a kind of camp version of expressionism .

369
00:19:22,328 --> 00:19:25,992
A kind of camp Gothic with everything a bit larger than life .

370
00:19:26,082 --> 00:19:30,417
Huge vertical spaces , diagonal beams , lots of shadows .

371
00:19:30,670 --> 00:19:34,254
And this became one of the trademarks of Charles D. Hall , or Danny Hall .

372
00:19:34,340 --> 00:19:37,002
He was an architect from Norwich , England ,

373
00:19:37,093 --> 00:19:39,011
who had gone over to Canada with his brother .

374
00:19:39,095 --> 00:19:42,258
His brother became construction manager at Universal Studios ,

375
00:19:42,348 --> 00:19:46,512
and Danny Hall became the overall art director or design manager of the studio .

376
00:19:46,852 --> 00:19:50,314
And apparently , they used to have arguments on the set because the brother would say ,

377
00:19:50,398 --> 00:19:52,482
" I can 't make it like that , I 'm going to make it like this , "

378
00:19:52,566 --> 00:19:56,059
and alter the designs as he made them . But it was all in good part .

379
00:19:56,445 --> 00:20:00,439
Hall had cut his teeth in the ' 20s on The Phantom of the Opera ,

380
00:20:00,574 --> 00:20:03,869
where he contributed to the opera house , the famous opera house set ,

381
00:20:03,953 --> 00:20:05,370
and The Hunchback of Notre Dame ,

382
00:20:05,454 --> 00:20:07,956
where he contributed to the great set of Notre Dame Cathedral ,

383
00:20:08,040 --> 00:20:12,329
but I think most of his ideas came from the German director Paul Leni ,

384
00:20:12,420 --> 00:20:16,664
who came over to Universal in 1927 and directed The Cat and the Canary ,

385
00:20:16,757 --> 00:20:19,249
which Charles Danny Hall designed .

386
00:20:19,343 --> 00:20:22,381
So , the visualization is such a strong aspect of this

387
00:20:22,471 --> 00:20:25,680
and set a kind of brand image for Universal Studios

388
00:20:25,766 --> 00:20:29,304
at a time when the studios were finding a visual corporate image ,

389
00:20:29,395 --> 00:20:32,763
the big white set of MGM , the Bauhaus of Paramount .

390
00:20:32,982 --> 00:20:34,893
Well , Danny Hall 's Gothic

391
00:20:34,984 --> 00:20:39,399
became the corporate image of Universal , and remained so really until the late ' 30s ,

392
00:20:39,488 --> 00:20:41,900
when the Laemmles sold the studio .

393
00:20:42,825 --> 00:20:44,441
You said I was crazy .

394
00:20:45,327 --> 00:20:48,365
- Tomorrow we 'll see about that . - [ SHOUTS ] Don 't touch that !

395
00:20:48,539 --> 00:20:50,530
[ THUNDER RUMBLING ]

396
00:20:53,627 --> 00:20:54,627
I 'm sorry , Doctor ...

397
00:20:54,712 --> 00:20:57,631
FRAYLING : James Whale wrote , " I consider the creation of The Monster

398
00:20:57,715 --> 00:20:58,924
" to be the high spot of the film "

399
00:20:59,008 --> 00:21:01,802
" because if the audience didn 't believe the thing had really been made , "

400
00:21:01,886 --> 00:21:04,763
" they wouldn 't be bothered with what it was supposed to do afterwards . "

401
00:21:04,847 --> 00:21:07,680
" Frankenstein merely has to believe what he sees , "

402
00:21:07,808 --> 00:21:10,095
" which is all we ask the audience to do . "

403
00:21:10,186 --> 00:21:13,804
When Whale wrote to Colin Clive asking if he 'd take the part ,

404
00:21:13,898 --> 00:21:16,936
they 'd worked together on the play and film Journey 's End ,

405
00:21:17,026 --> 00:21:22,612
where Clive played this over-the-top , tense , shrill Captain Stanhope ,

406
00:21:22,698 --> 00:21:25,986
who is shell-shocked in his dugout in the First World War

407
00:21:26,076 --> 00:21:27,566
and has become an alcoholic .

408
00:21:27,661 --> 00:21:30,574
Whale wrote to him , " Frankenstein is a grand part

409
00:21:30,664 --> 00:21:33,041
" and I think it will fit you as well as Stanhope .

410
00:21:33,125 --> 00:21:37,414
" I see Frankenstein as an intensely sane person , but at times rather fanatical

411
00:21:37,505 --> 00:21:39,872
" and in one or two scenes , a little hysterical .

412
00:21:40,049 --> 00:21:44,543
" In the first scene in the lab , he becomes very conscious of the theatrical drama ,

413
00:21:44,678 --> 00:21:46,464
" and goes a little insane about it .

414
00:21:46,597 --> 00:21:49,259
" There are none of Dracula 's maniacal cackles .

415
00:21:49,350 --> 00:21:53,270
" I want it all to be very modern , materialistic treatment of this medieval story ,

416
00:21:53,354 --> 00:21:56,312
" something of Dr. Caligari , something of Edgar Allan Poe

417
00:21:56,398 --> 00:21:58,560
" and , of course , a good deal of us . "

418
00:21:58,651 --> 00:22:02,189
" Us " being the relationship they 'd developed when making Journey 's End

419
00:22:02,279 --> 00:22:05,397
on the London stage , the New York stage and in the film ,

420
00:22:05,533 --> 00:22:07,117
which had been made a couple of years before ,

421
00:22:07,201 --> 00:22:09,851
and which got James Whale his contract with Universal .

422
00:22:10,246 --> 00:22:14,240
But it 's the theatricality of this that is so surprising , in a way .

423
00:22:14,333 --> 00:22:16,877
Frankenstein is setting up the scene , both for the audience

424
00:22:16,961 --> 00:22:19,248
and for Waldman , Victor and Elizabeth .

425
00:22:19,338 --> 00:22:24,003
He sets up the chairs so that Elizabeth , Waldman and Victor can watch ,

426
00:22:24,093 --> 00:22:26,630
and Frankenstein talks about , " Watch the show , "

427
00:22:26,720 --> 00:22:28,138
in fact , in the shooting script , he says ,

428
00:22:28,222 --> 00:22:31,681
" The overture begins , " and you get this overture of thunder .

429
00:22:31,767 --> 00:22:35,353
No music , there 's no music in this film at all , which is very surprising .

430
00:22:35,437 --> 00:22:38,190
There 's a piece of specially written music behind the credits ,

431
00:22:38,274 --> 00:22:41,733
a misterioso piece , and a piece of music at the end credits ,

432
00:22:41,819 --> 00:22:45,687
which was an off-the-shelf piece from silent cinema , an appassionato ,

433
00:22:45,781 --> 00:22:49,274
but it 's thunder , it 's natural sounds during the actual film .

434
00:22:49,952 --> 00:22:53,445
One man crazy , and there 's the audience watching him .

435
00:22:53,664 --> 00:22:57,453
And so , the audience sitting in the laboratory becomes a surrogate

436
00:22:57,543 --> 00:22:59,093
for the audience in the cinema .

437
00:22:59,336 --> 00:23:00,545
- Watch the show . - [ THUNDER RUMBLING ]

438
00:23:00,629 --> 00:23:01,289
- Watch the show . - [ THUNDER RUMBLING ]

439
00:23:01,380 --> 00:23:03,246
Get the batteries .

440
00:23:03,382 --> 00:23:05,634
FRAYLING : This , I suppose , is the most famous sequence

441
00:23:05,718 --> 00:23:07,049
in the entire film ,

442
00:23:07,219 --> 00:23:09,961
where the designs of Danny Hall and Herman Rosse ,

443
00:23:10,055 --> 00:23:14,094
the bits of engineering bric-a-brac from Kenneth Strickfaden ,

444
00:23:14,184 --> 00:23:17,176
the tense , hysterical performance of Colin Clive ,

445
00:23:17,271 --> 00:23:19,439
a long way away from the young research student ,

446
00:23:19,523 --> 00:23:22,811
the cool , young research student in Mary Shelley 's original novel .

447
00:23:22,902 --> 00:23:25,894
This is a much more mature figure , kind of manic old Etonian

448
00:23:25,988 --> 00:23:29,231
who chain-smokes and goes completely over-the-top like Stanhope .

449
00:23:29,325 --> 00:23:32,244
Instead of the First World War , it 's science that 's turned his head ,

450
00:23:32,328 --> 00:23:34,239
although he is completely sane .

451
00:23:34,330 --> 00:23:39,450
And Fritz , scurrying around as the laboratory assistant , the puffer ,

452
00:23:39,543 --> 00:23:41,284
all these things come together .

453
00:23:44,340 --> 00:23:49,005
In the script , it says that the table is lifted 12 to 15 feet above floor level ,

454
00:23:49,094 --> 00:23:52,587
but just look at this , right up into the roof of the watchtower .

455
00:23:52,681 --> 00:23:56,140
A hugely vertical set . Very , very large .

456
00:23:56,226 --> 00:23:59,435
James Whale and Danny Hall were really thinking on a grand scale

457
00:23:59,521 --> 00:24:00,682
of these sets .

458
00:24:00,814 --> 00:24:02,475
[ THUNDER RUMBLING ]

459
00:24:03,734 --> 00:24:08,479
" It says in the script , " Sparks crackle , rotary sparks make snapping circles of fire .

460
00:24:09,698 --> 00:24:14,033
" Great electrical display on glass rings around the table crackle with intense heat . "

461
00:24:14,870 --> 00:24:20,991
And , in the script , the idea is that the corpse lying on the surgical table

462
00:24:21,085 --> 00:24:23,085
is all black when it goes up in the air ,

463
00:24:23,337 --> 00:24:25,578
and when it comes down , it 's gone white .

464
00:24:25,714 --> 00:24:27,591
A symbol of the life being brought into it .

465
00:24:27,675 --> 00:24:29,837
This isn 't evident from the film .

466
00:24:29,927 --> 00:24:33,420
Instead , you get the famous movement of the hand .

467
00:24:38,769 --> 00:24:41,062
Also in the script , Elizabeth passes out at this point

468
00:24:41,146 --> 00:24:42,807
in the excitement of it all ,

469
00:24:42,898 --> 00:24:45,066
but she seems to somehow keep control of herself

470
00:24:45,150 --> 00:24:46,900
[ CHUCKLES ] during the film version .

471
00:24:49,530 --> 00:24:51,146
And now , " It 's alive . "

472
00:24:51,240 --> 00:24:55,484
In 1823 , it was , " ' It lives , ' said Frankenstein . "

473
00:24:55,744 --> 00:24:58,580
" It 's alive . " He 's supposed to say it twice in the script ,

474
00:24:58,664 --> 00:25:00,081
he says it seven times in the film .

475
00:25:00,165 --> 00:25:03,908
" It 's alive , it 's alive , " building up into this hysteria

476
00:25:04,003 --> 00:25:05,253
that James Whale mentioned .

477
00:25:05,337 --> 00:25:07,297
Partly ' cause he 's playing up to the audience ,

478
00:25:07,381 --> 00:25:10,214
partly because this is the great moment of his life .

479
00:25:10,759 --> 00:25:12,750
Now I know what it feels like ...

480
00:25:12,845 --> 00:25:14,429
FRAYLING : " Now I know what it feels like to be God , "

481
00:25:14,513 --> 00:25:15,753
very controversial line .

482
00:25:15,889 --> 00:25:18,130
Cut in some places on first release

483
00:25:18,225 --> 00:25:21,684
and in all places in 1937 when it was re-released .

484
00:25:21,770 --> 00:25:24,814
That line was thought to be too much , and has only recently been put back .

485
00:25:24,898 --> 00:25:29,938
It was covered by a peal of thunder in the version that I saw in the 1960s ,

486
00:25:30,029 --> 00:25:33,657
and only recently has " Now I know what it feels like to be God " come back .

487
00:25:33,741 --> 00:25:35,200
And after the intensity of that ,

488
00:25:35,284 --> 00:25:38,777
another over-lit domestic scene and change of mood .

489
00:25:39,121 --> 00:25:41,613
James Whale 's personal taste in interior design

490
00:25:41,707 --> 00:25:46,076
with lots of silver , lots of flowers , lots of candelabra , lots of paneling .

491
00:25:46,170 --> 00:25:47,626
He liked that sort of design ,

492
00:25:47,713 --> 00:25:51,456
and tends to have his domestic scenes in those sort of settings ,

493
00:25:51,550 --> 00:25:53,632
in this case with mullioned windows .

494
00:25:53,761 --> 00:25:57,880
And the old Baron , because Frankenstein is the son of a baron ,

495
00:25:57,973 --> 00:26:01,273
he 's gone up market since the novel , where he 's simply a scientist .

496
00:26:01,769 --> 00:26:05,137
The Baron is played by Frederick Kerr , 73 years old ,

497
00:26:05,314 --> 00:26:08,978
old-style British actor who played the father , the deaf father ,

498
00:26:09,234 --> 00:26:11,817
In Whale 's previous film , Waterloo Bridge .

499
00:26:11,904 --> 00:26:16,148
Cantankerous , bucolic , ruddy , portly ,

500
00:26:16,241 --> 00:26:19,905
and lots of extra comedy added after the shooting script stage

501
00:26:19,995 --> 00:26:22,539
in order to turn him into even more of a burlesque character .

502
00:26:22,623 --> 00:26:26,833
He 's also a bully , and there 's a sub-theme running through Frankenstein

503
00:26:26,919 --> 00:26:30,708
which Whale emphasized , of how the father is very tyrannical

504
00:26:30,798 --> 00:26:35,543
and the young scientist is rather desperate to impress him ,

505
00:26:35,636 --> 00:26:37,047
but is unable to do so .

506
00:26:37,262 --> 00:26:38,923
That isn 't in the script at all ,

507
00:26:39,014 --> 00:26:41,802
but it seems to have emerged at the last minute .

508
00:26:41,892 --> 00:26:45,977
Frankenstein says later in the film , " My father never believes in anyone , "

509
00:26:46,063 --> 00:26:47,563
whereas in the script it said ,

510
00:26:47,648 --> 00:26:50,448
" My father will believe when the rest of the world does . "

511
00:26:50,859 --> 00:26:52,475
Thank you , Herr Vogel .

512
00:26:53,278 --> 00:26:55,989
Both in my private and official capacities as Burgomaster ...

513
00:26:56,073 --> 00:26:58,867
FRAYLING : And this piece of officialdom , in the form of the Burgomaster ,

514
00:26:58,951 --> 00:27:01,283
who comes in to be pushed around by the Baron ,

515
00:27:01,662 --> 00:27:04,905
and you get this comedy of eccentricity , comedy of manners ,

516
00:27:05,290 --> 00:27:07,247
comedy of social gradations .

517
00:27:07,334 --> 00:27:09,826
James Whale was a bit of a snob .

518
00:27:09,920 --> 00:27:13,256
He was himself from a working class family in Dudley , Worcestershire ,

519
00:27:13,340 --> 00:27:16,549
the son of a blast-furnace man and a nurse ,

520
00:27:16,677 --> 00:27:19,009
and he had many , many brothers and sisters .

521
00:27:19,221 --> 00:27:22,714
And when he came to Hollywood , he remade his curriculum vitae .

522
00:27:22,808 --> 00:27:26,269
Shortly after Frankenstein came out , he reduced several years off his age

523
00:27:26,353 --> 00:27:30,103
so he went from his 40s into his 30s . He claimed that he had a private tutor

524
00:27:30,190 --> 00:27:33,526
and he liked to look up his family tree to see if there was any nobility

525
00:27:33,610 --> 00:27:36,568
hidden in the Whale family in the dim and distant past .

526
00:27:36,655 --> 00:27:39,238
So social things meant a lot to James Whale ,

527
00:27:39,366 --> 00:27:41,326
and this comedy of manners has a kind of edge to it .

528
00:27:41,410 --> 00:27:43,777
I mean , the Baron is pushing around officialdom .

529
00:27:43,871 --> 00:27:47,040
He 's only doing his job , after all , but that becomes a comedy issue .

530
00:27:47,124 --> 00:27:49,536
And this is like a West End play

531
00:27:49,626 --> 00:27:52,744
or a Broadway play of a certain kind in the 1920s ,

532
00:27:52,838 --> 00:27:55,381
where comedy of manners was the heart of the comedy .

533
00:27:55,465 --> 00:27:56,508
What do you mean , " No , no " ?

534
00:27:56,592 --> 00:27:58,301
- What about his work ? - Stuff and nonsense !

535
00:27:58,385 --> 00:27:59,967
What about his wedding ?

536
00:28:00,095 --> 00:28:01,927
There is another woman ,

537
00:28:02,931 --> 00:28:04,968
and I 'm going to find her .

538
00:28:07,769 --> 00:28:10,063
FRAYLING : In the script , we then go back to the laboratory ,

539
00:28:10,147 --> 00:28:13,435
where Frankenstein is pacing up and down in a nervous way ,

540
00:28:13,525 --> 00:28:15,401
where you see the creature for the first time ,

541
00:28:15,485 --> 00:28:19,274
the " creature " in the novel , the " monster " in the film from the word go ,

542
00:28:19,364 --> 00:28:22,652
whereas in the film , it 's Waldman that 's pacing up and down .

543
00:28:22,868 --> 00:28:26,077
But a nice design touch , the equipment is covered in white sheets

544
00:28:26,163 --> 00:28:29,872
as if there are ghosts standing behind the two scientists ,

545
00:28:30,042 --> 00:28:31,749
the ghosts of the previous scene .

546
00:28:31,877 --> 00:28:34,377
That 's not mentioned at all on the shooting script .

547
00:28:35,255 --> 00:28:36,711
Poor old Waldman .

548
00:28:36,798 --> 00:28:39,717
FRAYLING : In a famous speech coming up which isn 't on the shooting script at all ,

549
00:28:39,801 --> 00:28:41,758
which many think was added by Whale ,

550
00:28:41,845 --> 00:28:44,712
the great speech about , " Don 't you ever overreach , "

551
00:28:44,806 --> 00:28:46,391
" don 't you ever try to push the envelope , "

552
00:28:46,475 --> 00:28:48,807
" don 't you ever try to do something dangerous ? "

553
00:28:49,144 --> 00:28:50,726
" Why are you so conventional ? "

554
00:28:50,812 --> 00:28:54,100
And people have reckoned that this maybe is James Whale speaking ,

555
00:28:54,191 --> 00:28:58,059
it could equally be a focus on the theme of overreaching ,

556
00:28:58,153 --> 00:29:01,236
the Promethean theme in the film , the Faustian theme ,

557
00:29:01,323 --> 00:29:03,781
actually distilled into a serious speech .

558
00:29:03,867 --> 00:29:05,577
Whale obviously thought this was the moment

559
00:29:05,661 --> 00:29:10,076
to do a head-on , close-up speech about the dangers of overreaching ,

560
00:29:10,165 --> 00:29:14,784
Icarus getting his wings singed , Faust blowing himself up ,

561
00:29:15,003 --> 00:29:18,337
Adam eating the fruit of the tree of knowledge .

562
00:29:18,507 --> 00:29:22,717
Critics of this kind of philosophy of science , if I can put it that way ,

563
00:29:22,803 --> 00:29:26,762
have often complained that films like this put people off a scientific career

564
00:29:26,848 --> 00:29:30,261
and create a sort of anti-science attitude in society .

565
00:29:30,352 --> 00:29:32,812
But actually , there 's rather a marvelous by-product

566
00:29:32,896 --> 00:29:34,689
of the laboratory scene we 've just seen .

567
00:29:34,773 --> 00:29:38,516
Dr. Jean Rosenbaum of New Mexico later admitted

568
00:29:39,027 --> 00:29:43,271
that he first got the idea of the cardiac pacemaker , his invention ,

569
00:29:43,365 --> 00:29:47,233
it came to him in 1951 when he recalled being scared as a child

570
00:29:47,327 --> 00:29:49,864
by the laboratory scene in Frankenstein .

571
00:29:49,955 --> 00:29:53,789
Absurd as this drama may be to sophisticated audiences of today ,

572
00:29:53,875 --> 00:29:58,836
it put in mind this idea of sparking a heart into life .

573
00:29:58,922 --> 00:30:01,254
And so , one of the spin-offs of the lab scene

574
00:30:01,341 --> 00:30:03,878
is the cardiac pacemaker .

575
00:30:04,177 --> 00:30:07,169
Overreaching sometimes leads in interesting directions .

576
00:30:07,264 --> 00:30:08,754
... and it will destroy you !

577
00:30:09,057 --> 00:30:10,843
Patience , patience .

578
00:30:12,019 --> 00:30:15,146
FRAYLING : And of course , this is the moment when Frankenstein discovers

579
00:30:15,230 --> 00:30:18,643
that Fritz has put the wrong brain , this eugenic theme ,

580
00:30:18,734 --> 00:30:20,224
into his creature .

581
00:30:20,319 --> 00:30:23,321
And there 's that wonderful double-take as Colin Clive realizes

582
00:30:23,405 --> 00:30:27,569
that despite his best intentions , this is likely to go seriously wrong .

583
00:30:27,659 --> 00:30:29,494
And there 's an interesting ambiguity about this ,

584
00:30:29,578 --> 00:30:34,163
because in the John Balderston version and the Robert Florey outline ,

585
00:30:34,499 --> 00:30:37,582
the monster comes out as a ravening , grunting beast

586
00:30:37,669 --> 00:30:40,411
who really doesn 't have any redeeming features .

587
00:30:40,505 --> 00:30:43,167
He 's got this dysfunctio cerebri sewn into him ,

588
00:30:43,258 --> 00:30:45,499
so he 's going to be a complete psychopath .

589
00:30:45,594 --> 00:30:48,513
But actually , Boris Karloff played it , under Whale 's direction ,

590
00:30:48,597 --> 00:30:49,337
But actually , Boris Karloff played it , under Whale 's direction ,

591
00:30:49,431 --> 00:30:50,887
as a sympathetic portrayal .

592
00:30:50,974 --> 00:30:56,094
So although he 's got this terrible start in life , there 's also sympathy for the character ,

593
00:30:56,188 --> 00:30:58,688
which , of course , made that theme much more subtle .

594
00:30:59,191 --> 00:31:02,568
" In the script , " The door slowly opens , revealing The Monster in the frame .

595
00:31:02,652 --> 00:31:06,361
" The Monster is a strangely hideous , grotesque , inhuman figure , "

596
00:31:06,448 --> 00:31:08,314
" only the semblance of humanity . "

597
00:31:09,034 --> 00:31:12,447
And here we have a series of close-ups by Whale

598
00:31:12,913 --> 00:31:18,079
to introduce us to this astonishing image of Boris Karloff with his makeup .

599
00:31:19,628 --> 00:31:22,528
The bolt through his neck , which is what you plug him into ,

600
00:31:22,798 --> 00:31:26,009
the sawn-off cranium , flat top with the hair brushed forward over it ,

601
00:31:26,093 --> 00:31:27,843
which is how they put his brain in .

602
00:31:27,928 --> 00:31:31,887
The sunken cheeks , the wonderful doe-like eyes ,

603
00:31:31,973 --> 00:31:33,973
and the way he walks by leaning forward .

604
00:31:34,059 --> 00:31:37,177
It says in the script , " He must not walk like a robot . "

605
00:31:37,270 --> 00:31:40,888
And one of the design ideas for the creature , as late as 1930 ,

606
00:31:40,982 --> 00:31:42,472
by Universal artists ,

607
00:31:42,567 --> 00:31:46,060
was , in fact , a robot , a mechanical monster ,

608
00:31:46,238 --> 00:31:48,900
or a Neanderthal sort of figure ,

609
00:31:48,990 --> 00:31:51,607
or maybe someone whose brains were on the outside ,

610
00:31:51,701 --> 00:31:55,538
a sort of bug-eyed monster or an alien figure , maybe The Monster should look like that .

611
00:31:55,622 --> 00:31:58,740
But no , he has this extraordinary sympathetic look ,

612
00:31:59,376 --> 00:32:03,870
which enables Karloff 's facial features to be as mobile as they need to be

613
00:32:03,964 --> 00:32:05,256
even though there 's makeup

614
00:32:05,340 --> 00:32:09,129
that took three and a half hours to apply every morning on top of it .

615
00:32:09,845 --> 00:32:13,930
Jack Pierce , the makeup man , the head of makeup at Universal ,

616
00:32:14,015 --> 00:32:17,007
who was responsible for the makeup , with James Whale ,

617
00:32:17,102 --> 00:32:19,264
talked about how he got a lot of his ideas

618
00:32:19,354 --> 00:32:22,563
from anatomical books and medical books and scientific research .

619
00:32:23,650 --> 00:32:26,861
And he actually did research on what happens to bodies after decomposition

620
00:32:26,945 --> 00:32:30,573
and one of the things that happens is that a lot of liquid goes into the extremities ,

621
00:32:30,657 --> 00:32:32,614
so you get big hands and big feet .

622
00:32:32,701 --> 00:32:35,203
And people writing about the mummies of ancient Egypt

623
00:32:35,287 --> 00:32:38,746
had said that that 's one of the by-products of being buried .

624
00:32:38,832 --> 00:32:43,326
So , Karloff has these big hands and big feet with the asphalt spreader 's boots

625
00:32:43,420 --> 00:32:47,664
and he almost looks like a sort of strange American working man ,

626
00:32:47,757 --> 00:32:50,795
except that he 's wearing a suit that 's too small for him

627
00:32:50,886 --> 00:32:53,137
that 's supposed to be a cast-off from Frankenstein .

628
00:32:53,221 --> 00:32:56,304
And from the medical textbooks ,

629
00:32:56,850 --> 00:33:00,468
he looks also as though he 's got an acromegalic condition .

630
00:33:00,562 --> 00:33:03,054
An image of disability rather than monstrosity ,

631
00:33:03,148 --> 00:33:07,312
which I suspect that Jack Pierce used when developing his makeup .

632
00:33:07,652 --> 00:33:09,821
Boris Karloff had been in Hollywood for 10 years ,

633
00:33:09,905 --> 00:33:13,398
had appeared in over 80 movies . His real name was William Henry Pratt .

634
00:33:13,492 --> 00:33:15,403
He was born in Dulwich , England ,

635
00:33:15,494 --> 00:33:18,371
and most recently was appearing in a gangster film called Graft ,

636
00:33:18,455 --> 00:33:21,805
which is why he was on the Universal lot when James Whale found him .

637
00:33:22,000 --> 00:33:24,043
And he 'd also appeared in The Criminal Code

638
00:33:24,127 --> 00:33:27,540
as a murdering jailbird in Howard Hawks ' film .

639
00:33:27,631 --> 00:33:29,841
But he really wasn 't making a success of his career ,

640
00:33:29,925 --> 00:33:32,257
and this , for him , was the turning point .

641
00:33:36,014 --> 00:33:38,756
A wonderful piece of pantomime with the hands

642
00:33:39,059 --> 00:33:42,812
grasping towards the light of the skylight , again this theme of light and flame .

643
00:33:42,896 --> 00:33:46,230
There 's good light , the light that gave him the vital spark ,

644
00:33:46,316 --> 00:33:48,526
the light of the heavens , the light of the sun ,

645
00:33:48,610 --> 00:33:51,010
and there 's bad light , the light of the flambeau ,

646
00:33:51,112 --> 00:33:54,525
the light of the gravedigger 's pipe , the light of the flaming torch ,

647
00:33:54,616 --> 00:33:58,029
the light of the mill at the end as The Monster gets destroyed .

648
00:33:58,286 --> 00:33:59,993
Good fire and bad fire .

649
00:34:00,121 --> 00:34:03,739
It 's a kind of visualization of the theme of the modern Prometheus .

650
00:34:04,167 --> 00:34:07,501
In the shooting script , which was dated August , 1931 ,

651
00:34:07,587 --> 00:34:09,203
so it was a very recent one ,

652
00:34:09,297 --> 00:34:12,631
the fire , in fact , comes from matches being struck on the floor

653
00:34:12,717 --> 00:34:17,382
and the lighting of cigarettes , which causes The Monster to go haywire .

654
00:34:17,472 --> 00:34:19,891
And Frankenstein keeps striking matches on the floor

655
00:34:19,975 --> 00:34:21,142
in order to keep him at bay .

656
00:34:21,226 --> 00:34:22,268
But the flaming torch is a sort of medieval concept .

657
00:34:22,352 --> 00:34:23,352
But the flaming torch is a sort of medieval concept .

658
00:34:23,436 --> 00:34:26,272
Again this timelessness , you know , " What sort of period are we in ? "

659
00:34:26,356 --> 00:34:27,721
Is brought in instead .

660
00:34:28,316 --> 00:34:30,057
[ CLATTERING ]

661
00:34:30,151 --> 00:34:33,064
Now we 're down in the very expressionist cellar

662
00:34:33,154 --> 00:34:34,519
of the watchtower .

663
00:34:34,614 --> 00:34:38,198
Lots of diagonals , lots of beams , lots of slanting walls .

664
00:34:38,577 --> 00:34:40,377
A world that 's about to totter over ,

665
00:34:40,537 --> 00:34:43,154
and Fritz is tormenting the creature with a whip .

666
00:34:43,248 --> 00:34:46,709
In the Balderston version , it was Frankenstein that tormented the creature ,

667
00:34:46,793 --> 00:34:49,785
but actually , Frankenstein is holding Fritz back .

668
00:34:52,716 --> 00:34:54,548
Oh , come away , Fritz .

669
00:34:54,843 --> 00:34:56,174
FRAYLING : Sad moment .

670
00:34:56,511 --> 00:34:58,752
" Leave it alone . Leave it alone . "

671
00:34:58,847 --> 00:35:01,197
Again , a line that was added at the last minute .

672
00:35:01,516 --> 00:35:04,929
Colin Clive being very touching , realizing that his life 's work

673
00:35:05,020 --> 00:35:06,806
is going very seriously wrong .

674
00:35:07,272 --> 00:35:09,809
The censors were worried about the close-ups

675
00:35:09,983 --> 00:35:12,190
of the tormenting by Fritz of the creature

676
00:35:12,277 --> 00:35:15,565
and these were trimmed when the film actually went on release

677
00:35:15,655 --> 00:35:17,316
at the end of 1931 .

678
00:35:17,407 --> 00:35:19,257
It was thought to be going a bit far .

679
00:35:22,621 --> 00:35:25,414
In the shooting script , there 's another scene here set in the village

680
00:35:25,498 --> 00:35:28,125
where you had the villagers scurrying around the main street

681
00:35:28,209 --> 00:35:32,077
hearing the howls of the creature and the noises from the cellar

682
00:35:32,255 --> 00:35:34,747
and looking up at the watchtower on the hill ,

683
00:35:34,841 --> 00:35:37,754
and realizing that something very strange is going on .

684
00:35:37,844 --> 00:35:41,212
But in the film , we cut straight to Waldman and Frankenstein

685
00:35:41,306 --> 00:35:44,549
rushing down into the cellar , down those stone steps

686
00:35:44,643 --> 00:35:48,136
which Fritz had had so much comedy coming down earlier ,

687
00:35:49,522 --> 00:35:51,638
and trying to see what 's going on .

688
00:35:51,733 --> 00:35:54,020
It 's the death of Fritz , who 's hung up .

689
00:35:54,110 --> 00:35:57,569
In England , the censor was very worried about this shot

690
00:35:57,656 --> 00:35:59,991
of seeing Fritz actually hanging from the cellar ,

691
00:36:00,075 --> 00:36:01,565
and it was trimmed .

692
00:36:02,077 --> 00:36:03,408
[ SNARLING ]

693
00:36:04,704 --> 00:36:07,913
Danny Hall 's design , Herman Rosse 's design ,

694
00:36:07,999 --> 00:36:11,788
are really an embodiment of what the script says about the cellar ,

695
00:36:11,878 --> 00:36:14,119
" Dark , old beams , sagging a little with age , "

696
00:36:14,214 --> 00:36:15,375
" support the low roof . "

697
00:36:15,465 --> 00:36:17,508
" The walls are damp and covered with fungus . "

698
00:36:17,592 --> 00:36:20,209
" A suitable place for the creature to live in . "

699
00:36:20,303 --> 00:36:24,092
And Karloff , whose performance is so extraordinary and sympathetic

700
00:36:24,182 --> 00:36:26,890
as the creature , even while he 's raving in this way ,

701
00:36:26,976 --> 00:36:28,808
actually said about his performance ,

702
00:36:28,895 --> 00:36:30,980
" This was a pathetic creature who , like us all , "

703
00:36:31,064 --> 00:36:33,271
" had neither wish nor say in his creation , "

704
00:36:33,358 --> 00:36:36,225
" and certainly did not wish upon itself the hideous image "

705
00:36:36,319 --> 00:36:39,903
" which automatically terrified humans whom it tried to befriend . "

706
00:36:39,989 --> 00:36:42,867
He much preferred it to be called " creature " rather than " monster . "

707
00:36:42,951 --> 00:36:45,784
And in fact , in Mary Shelley 's original novel ,

708
00:36:45,870 --> 00:36:50,910
the epigraph on the front page was a quote from Milton 's Paradise Lost ,

709
00:36:51,084 --> 00:36:54,873
" Did I request thee , Maker , from my clay To mold me Man ? "

710
00:36:54,963 --> 00:36:57,125
" Did I ask to be brought into the world ? "

711
00:36:57,549 --> 00:37:00,635
As Karloff said , it was as though man , in his blundering , searching attempts

712
00:37:00,719 --> 00:37:04,758
to improve himself , was to find himself deserted by his God .

713
00:37:04,889 --> 00:37:08,757
Karloff didn 't ask to be created , and he 's been deserted by his creator ,

714
00:37:08,852 --> 00:37:11,059
who won 't take responsibility for him .

715
00:37:11,146 --> 00:37:13,147
He 's taken one look at him and he 's run for it .

716
00:37:13,231 --> 00:37:15,723
And now , they 've got to do damage limitation .

717
00:37:15,942 --> 00:37:17,485
And this is one of the great subtleties ,

718
00:37:17,569 --> 00:37:20,119
people have , again , projected this onto James Whale ,

719
00:37:20,488 --> 00:37:23,981
gay , an outsider , a very theatrical sort of character ,

720
00:37:24,075 --> 00:37:25,361
rather shy inside ,

721
00:37:25,452 --> 00:37:27,536
and maybe he was identifying with the creature .

722
00:37:27,620 --> 00:37:30,081
Well , maybe he was , maybe he wasn 't , it 's in the original novel ,

723
00:37:30,165 --> 00:37:31,707
and it 's one of the great performances ,

724
00:37:31,791 --> 00:37:34,291
almost like a silent film performance or pantomime .

725
00:37:34,753 --> 00:37:37,088
The death of Fritz is interesting ' cause in the novel ,

726
00:37:37,172 --> 00:37:40,540
the creature is unable to have a family of his own .

727
00:37:40,633 --> 00:37:42,670
He 's the product of a cybernetic birth .

728
00:37:43,344 --> 00:37:47,008
He has a father , but no mother , he 's been created by the father .

729
00:37:47,098 --> 00:37:51,012
And in revenge for this , he gets his own back on Frankenstein 's family .

730
00:37:51,102 --> 00:37:53,552
He murders Frankenstein 's little brother , William ,

731
00:37:53,688 --> 00:37:55,898
which is the novel 's version of the Little Maria sequence

732
00:37:55,982 --> 00:37:59,270
that comes a little later , in a field just outside Geneva .

733
00:37:59,360 --> 00:38:01,821
He murders Frankenstein 's close friend , Henry Clerval ,

734
00:38:01,905 --> 00:38:04,897
and then he murders Elizabeth , his bride ,

735
00:38:04,991 --> 00:38:07,243
because in the book she has just become his bride ,

736
00:38:07,327 --> 00:38:08,494
and he murders her , as well .

737
00:38:08,578 --> 00:38:09,989
If he can 't have a family ,

738
00:38:10,079 --> 00:38:12,248
Frankenstein 's not going to be allowed one either .

739
00:38:12,332 --> 00:38:14,417
" I will be with you on your wedding night . "

740
00:38:14,501 --> 00:38:17,628
But in the film , it 's a more indiscriminate sort of murdering creature .

741
00:38:17,712 --> 00:38:21,922
He kills Fritz , and later on , Little Maria , as we 'll see ,

742
00:38:22,008 --> 00:38:25,717
and there 's no connection with the family in the way that the novel had .

743
00:38:25,804 --> 00:38:28,723
So some of the themes of the novel , this idea of cybernetic birth ,

744
00:38:28,807 --> 00:38:30,673
birthing , an allegory of birthing ...

745
00:38:31,059 --> 00:38:33,676
Mary Shelley had recently lost her own baby .

746
00:38:33,770 --> 00:38:36,979
All of that gets lost in ruthlessly paring it down

747
00:38:37,065 --> 00:38:40,649
to a story about the social responsibility of science

748
00:38:40,735 --> 00:38:42,317
for Depression audiences .

749
00:38:42,403 --> 00:38:44,940
One might wonder whether Depression audiences

750
00:38:45,031 --> 00:38:47,489
enjoyed a film like this that was so gloomy

751
00:38:47,575 --> 00:38:49,243
and the Motion Picture Herald , when it came out , said ,

752
00:38:49,327 --> 00:38:52,319
" People like the tragic best at those times "

753
00:38:52,413 --> 00:38:54,404
" when their own spirits are depressed . "

754
00:38:54,499 --> 00:38:57,084
" And the economists tell us that even more than their spirits "

755
00:38:57,168 --> 00:38:58,249
" are at a low ebb . "

756
00:38:58,336 --> 00:39:00,953
In other words , horror movies , this new genre ,

757
00:39:01,047 --> 00:39:05,541
might actually appeal to people who in real life are rather depressed .

758
00:39:05,635 --> 00:39:08,468
And maybe some of the themes of the film actually relate

759
00:39:08,555 --> 00:39:13,675
to the atmosphere in America in 1931 rather than in Geneva in 1816

760
00:39:13,768 --> 00:39:16,260
or in London when the play was produced in 1823 .

761
00:39:16,354 --> 00:39:19,597
This movie is as much about America at the time it was released ,

762
00:39:19,691 --> 00:39:22,485
the frustration of not being able to do something about it .

763
00:39:22,569 --> 00:39:24,570
You know , Karloff looks like an American working man

764
00:39:24,654 --> 00:39:27,698
and the sheer frustration of not being able to express himself

765
00:39:27,782 --> 00:39:29,364
is an important part of this .

766
00:39:29,450 --> 00:39:33,239
The experts have let us down , the people behind the stock market ,

767
00:39:33,329 --> 00:39:36,242
the economists , the policy-makers , they 've let us down ,

768
00:39:36,332 --> 00:39:38,375
just as Frankenstein lets down the creature .

769
00:39:38,459 --> 00:39:41,417
And there is a kind of allegory of depression going on here

770
00:39:41,504 --> 00:39:43,297
which would have no doubt have clicked with the audiences .

771
00:39:43,381 --> 00:39:45,299
And contemporary critics picked up on this .

772
00:39:45,383 --> 00:39:49,217
Of course , the articulacy of the creature is an interesting aspect of this .

773
00:39:49,304 --> 00:39:51,887
In all theatrical versions from 1823 onwards ,

774
00:39:51,973 --> 00:39:53,805
he 's a grunting monster .

775
00:39:54,058 --> 00:39:56,675
In the book , he 's very , very articulate .

776
00:39:56,811 --> 00:40:00,304
He stumbles around the woods and comes across a cottage ,

777
00:40:00,440 --> 00:40:03,899
where he watches the De Lacey family educating their children ,

778
00:40:03,985 --> 00:40:04,985
and he picks this up .

779
00:40:05,069 --> 00:40:07,071
It 's an early version of distance learning .

780
00:40:07,155 --> 00:40:10,068
And he picks up language and understanding

781
00:40:10,158 --> 00:40:11,865
and philosophy and argument

782
00:40:11,951 --> 00:40:14,067
and articulacy and morals

783
00:40:14,162 --> 00:40:15,994
from his watching of this family .

784
00:40:16,080 --> 00:40:18,947
He also finds a satchel , which has in it various books ,

785
00:40:19,042 --> 00:40:21,335
including Goethe 's The Sorrows of Young Werther ,

786
00:40:21,419 --> 00:40:22,409
Plutarch 's Lives ,

787
00:40:22,503 --> 00:40:26,588
and Milton 's Paradise Lost , of course . The fallen angel , forbidden knowledge .

788
00:40:26,674 --> 00:40:28,467
And after that , you can 't stop him talking .

789
00:40:28,551 --> 00:40:32,260
And there 's a famous meeting between the scientist and his creation

790
00:40:32,347 --> 00:40:36,261
on the Mer de Glace at Chamonix in the Alps in France ,

791
00:40:36,351 --> 00:40:38,388
where they talk moral philosophy .

792
00:40:38,478 --> 00:40:40,187
Well , in the film , all that 's cut out ,

793
00:40:40,271 --> 00:40:45,391
just like the dysfunctional brain cuts out a lot of the social responsibility theme ,

794
00:40:45,485 --> 00:40:48,022
ruthlessly pared down so he 's a grunting creature .

795
00:40:48,112 --> 00:40:50,854
Only in the second film , The Bride of Frankenstein ,

796
00:40:50,949 --> 00:40:56,035
will the Karloff character become articulate , and the De Lacey scene will be put back .

797
00:40:56,454 --> 00:40:57,944
It 's all my fault .

798
00:40:58,039 --> 00:41:00,576
- Get him on the sofa . - Come on .

799
00:41:02,126 --> 00:41:04,413
Oh . Oh , my boy .

800
00:41:08,883 --> 00:41:10,214
Thank you .

801
00:41:10,468 --> 00:41:12,803
FRAYLING : In the shooting script , there 's an interesting moment here

802
00:41:12,887 --> 00:41:17,632
where Waldman says to Frankenstein , " When you are well , we will write , "

803
00:41:17,725 --> 00:41:20,467
his research , " in a book for the world lo read . "

804
00:41:20,561 --> 00:41:22,646
" Yes , " says Frankenstein , " the world must know . "

805
00:41:22,730 --> 00:41:24,189
All of this must lead somewhere ,

806
00:41:24,273 --> 00:41:26,810
it must add to the sum total of human knowledge ,

807
00:41:26,901 --> 00:41:28,391
it must turn into science .

808
00:41:28,486 --> 00:41:31,569
All that went from the final version ,

809
00:41:31,656 --> 00:41:35,490
where there 's no sense that positive results are coming from this at all .

810
00:41:35,576 --> 00:41:37,442
[ PANTING ] No , I can 't . My work .

811
00:41:37,912 --> 00:41:39,914
What will happen to the records of my experiment ?

812
00:41:39,998 --> 00:41:41,284
We will preserve them .

813
00:41:41,416 --> 00:41:43,834
- And ... - I will see that it is painlessly destroyed .

814
00:41:43,918 --> 00:41:46,962
" We will preserve the records , " rather than " We 're going to write a book together . "

815
00:41:47,046 --> 00:41:49,333
Waldman is not that sympathetic a character

816
00:41:49,424 --> 00:41:51,800
that he 's actually going into partnership with Frankenstein .

817
00:41:51,884 --> 00:41:53,844
" He 's the guy who says , " Kill , kill , kill .

818
00:41:53,928 --> 00:41:57,046
" This is a nasty character , and the world isn 't ready for him . "

819
00:42:02,353 --> 00:42:08,520
In the novel , Waldman is in fact the progressive scientist at the medical institute ,

820
00:42:08,609 --> 00:42:12,694
the professor of chemistry , who actually encourages Frankenstein to overreach ,

821
00:42:12,780 --> 00:42:15,866
as opposed to the professor of natural philosophy , who holds him back .

822
00:42:15,950 --> 00:42:19,693
It 's Waldman who , in a lecture , says to the assembled students ,

823
00:42:19,787 --> 00:42:22,575
" The ancient teachers promised impossibilities , "

824
00:42:22,665 --> 00:42:24,747
" the modern masters promise very little . "

825
00:42:24,834 --> 00:42:27,041
" Why doesn 't modern science promise more ? "

826
00:42:27,128 --> 00:42:29,540
And this sows the seed in Frankenstein 's mind

827
00:42:29,630 --> 00:42:33,123
of overreaching and actually creating life .

828
00:42:34,093 --> 00:42:36,380
One thing in the text that isn 't clear

829
00:42:36,471 --> 00:42:40,385
is why the creature should be eight-foot-tall in the novel ,

830
00:42:40,475 --> 00:42:43,342
when , in fact , he 's been made out of pieces of bodies .

831
00:42:43,478 --> 00:42:45,139
And a clue to this is that

832
00:42:45,229 --> 00:42:47,937
Mary Shelley says that the body parts of the creature

833
00:42:48,024 --> 00:42:52,109
were found from dissecting rooms and graveyards ,

834
00:42:52,195 --> 00:42:55,404
and medical experiments and charnel houses ,

835
00:42:55,490 --> 00:42:57,731
but also from slaughterhouses .

836
00:42:57,825 --> 00:42:59,315
And it 's an interesting word

837
00:42:59,410 --> 00:43:03,080
because it suggests there 's animal parts inside the creature as well as human ones ,

838
00:43:03,164 --> 00:43:05,155
which might explain the strange scale .

839
00:43:05,249 --> 00:43:07,286
In the film , his boots are built up

840
00:43:07,376 --> 00:43:09,586
and he 's seven-foot-tall rather than eight-foot-tall ,

841
00:43:09,670 --> 00:43:11,707
but he 's obviously larger than life .

842
00:43:11,798 --> 00:43:13,048
Larger than life in every way ,

843
00:43:13,132 --> 00:43:17,091
as Mel Brooks made into the subject of lots of comedy in Young Frankenstein .

844
00:43:17,178 --> 00:43:19,263
But it 's one of the ambiguities of the novel .

845
00:43:19,347 --> 00:43:20,723
Another ambiguity in the novel

846
00:43:20,807 --> 00:43:23,517
is what Mary Shelley thought the creature should look like .

847
00:43:23,601 --> 00:43:26,138
The first thing she wrote was the laboratory scene .

848
00:43:26,229 --> 00:43:29,847
" It was in a dreary night of November that I beheld ... " Et cetera .

849
00:43:29,941 --> 00:43:32,526
But then she goes on to describe what the creature looks like .

850
00:43:32,610 --> 00:43:36,524
" His limbs were in proportion , and I 'd selected his features as beautiful . "

851
00:43:36,614 --> 00:43:37,979
" Beautiful ! Great God ! "

852
00:43:38,074 --> 00:43:41,785
" His yellow skin scarcely covered the work of muscles and arteries beneath , "

853
00:43:41,869 --> 00:43:46,488
" his hair was of a lustrous black and flowing , and his teeth of a pearly whiteness . "

854
00:43:46,582 --> 00:43:48,000
At one level , he 's very beautiful ,

855
00:43:48,084 --> 00:43:50,951
because his body parts have been selected with such care .

856
00:43:51,045 --> 00:43:52,581
But when you look at his eyes ,

857
00:43:52,672 --> 00:43:56,586
you see that there 's " a horrid contrast , " as she puts it , with his body .

858
00:43:56,676 --> 00:43:59,928
" His watery eyes are a mirror of his soul , " and there 's something ...

859
00:44:00,012 --> 00:44:00,971
Or has he got a soul ?

860
00:44:01,055 --> 00:44:04,058
And there 's something going on behind those eyes that 's not very nice .

861
00:44:04,142 --> 00:44:07,478
That 's all really quite subtle , and people notice there 's something wrong with him .

862
00:44:07,562 --> 00:44:10,600
And later on he 's described as getting increasingly ugly ,

863
00:44:10,690 --> 00:44:12,691
as an outward sign of how people treat him .

864
00:44:12,775 --> 00:44:15,187
In the film , again , a huge shortcut .

865
00:44:15,278 --> 00:44:17,029
He 's a bug-eyed monster from the word go .

866
00:44:17,113 --> 00:44:20,356
Not really bug-eyed , doe-eyed monster in Karloff 's performance .

867
00:44:20,449 --> 00:44:22,315
But that again is a kind of shortcut .

868
00:44:22,410 --> 00:44:23,952
And it turns the novel into something else ,

869
00:44:24,036 --> 00:44:26,869
from a piece of literature into a piece of mythology .

870
00:44:26,956 --> 00:44:29,323
I suppose Karloff 's makeup is up there

871
00:44:29,417 --> 00:44:32,002
with Charlie Chaplin 's bowler hat and Mickey Mouse 's ears

872
00:44:32,086 --> 00:44:34,703
as one of the great icons of Hollywood cinema ,

873
00:44:34,797 --> 00:44:36,913
recognizable anywhere in the world .

874
00:44:37,925 --> 00:44:41,134
- Let 's make it soon . - As soon as you like .

875
00:44:46,142 --> 00:44:49,436
FRAYLING : And now , after that darkness to light , to the villa , to the lake ,

876
00:44:49,520 --> 00:44:52,314
we could even be in Switzerland , like in the original story .

877
00:44:52,398 --> 00:44:54,685
And it 's all jollity for the wedding ,

878
00:44:54,775 --> 00:44:56,857
where these artificial orange blossoms

879
00:44:56,944 --> 00:45:01,154
are part of the Frankenstein family tradition on wedding days .

880
00:45:01,240 --> 00:45:02,407
And the little miniature ,

881
00:45:02,491 --> 00:45:05,324
which shows Baron and Baroness Frankenstein ,

882
00:45:05,411 --> 00:45:08,119
is the only reference to Frankenstein 's mother

883
00:45:08,206 --> 00:45:09,196
in the entire film .

884
00:45:09,290 --> 00:45:12,828
It 's a film about a motherless child , a cybernetic birth ,

885
00:45:12,919 --> 00:45:16,378
and the female is missing at all sorts of different levels .

886
00:45:16,464 --> 00:45:18,549
This little miniature is actually quite important .

887
00:45:18,633 --> 00:45:21,045
A lot of comedy with the Baron

888
00:45:21,135 --> 00:45:25,675
turned into even more of a cantankerous , sort of comedic character by James Whale .

889
00:45:25,765 --> 00:45:29,554
" And the whole gag about " My grandmother wouldn 't let him drink the wine

890
00:45:29,644 --> 00:45:32,062
" and therefore the wine has been saved in the cellar "

891
00:45:32,146 --> 00:45:33,772
was added by James Whale at the last minute .

892
00:45:33,856 --> 00:45:37,019
As was the maids all having a glass of champagne ,

893
00:45:37,109 --> 00:45:39,611
because the Baron doesn 't want to waste his best wine on them .

894
00:45:39,695 --> 00:45:41,811
The maids will appear at the very end

895
00:45:41,906 --> 00:45:44,616
in the happy epilogue that was added at the last minute .

896
00:45:44,700 --> 00:45:46,737
All of that came from James Whale .

897
00:45:46,827 --> 00:45:48,317
All full ? Yes ? Come along .

898
00:45:48,412 --> 00:45:52,656
Here 's a health to a son of the house of Frankenstein .

899
00:45:53,709 --> 00:45:54,710
FRAYLING : Son to the house of Frankenstein .

900
00:45:54,794 --> 00:45:58,128
The irony , as Frankenstein 's created this artificial son

901
00:45:58,214 --> 00:46:00,924
who is rampaging around the countryside while they speak .

902
00:46:01,008 --> 00:46:03,796
So the toast is , " A son to the house of Frankenstein . "

903
00:46:03,886 --> 00:46:07,299
And this irony is underlined several times from now on .

904
00:46:07,390 --> 00:46:12,601
And here we have a brightly lit set with paneling and flowers ,

905
00:46:12,687 --> 00:46:14,553
and champagne and champagne glasses .

906
00:46:14,647 --> 00:46:17,685
The other side of the design of the film Frankenstein .

907
00:46:17,775 --> 00:46:21,814
Darkness , expressionism , angularity , shadows .

908
00:46:21,904 --> 00:46:26,569
And then brightness , social comedy , eccentricity , snobbery ,

909
00:46:26,659 --> 00:46:30,072
and the staginess , theatricality .

910
00:46:30,162 --> 00:46:31,288
Whale absolutely loved that .

911
00:46:31,372 --> 00:46:32,362
Isn 't it wonderful ?

912
00:46:32,456 --> 00:46:36,084
And with the garlands of flowers and the baronial coats of arms up by the ceiling ,

913
00:46:36,168 --> 00:46:37,628
and a very , very cluttered set .

914
00:46:37,712 --> 00:46:41,671
Danny Hall was capable of doing these as well as the more expressionist ones .

915
00:46:41,882 --> 00:46:44,509
And now we 're out in the village for the first time in the film ,

916
00:46:44,593 --> 00:46:46,386
although not in the shooting script .

917
00:46:46,470 --> 00:46:50,224
And the village was designed by Danny Hall for All Quiet on the Western Front

918
00:46:50,308 --> 00:46:51,764
the year before Frankenstein .

919
00:46:51,851 --> 00:46:55,970
It was a standing set , a very large set , on the back lot at Universal ,

920
00:46:56,063 --> 00:46:57,428
a German village .

921
00:46:57,523 --> 00:46:59,149
And it was recycled for Frankenstein .

922
00:46:59,233 --> 00:47:03,477
And indeed used for all the subsequent Universal Frankenstein films as well .

923
00:47:05,072 --> 00:47:07,741
- Well , I suppose I 'd better show myself . - Make a little talk .

924
00:47:07,825 --> 00:47:09,691
[ CHURCH BELLS TOLLING ]

925
00:47:10,119 --> 00:47:11,575
[ ALL CHEERING ]

926
00:47:12,413 --> 00:47:15,405
FRAYLING : The bells peal out the joy of wedding time .

927
00:47:15,499 --> 00:47:18,669
We 've had the funeral bells at the beginning , this is the wedding bells ,

928
00:47:18,753 --> 00:47:22,667
and we 'll have a tolling bell of doom in the last sequence as well .

929
00:47:23,007 --> 00:47:25,669
In the absence of music , the use of sound ,

930
00:47:25,760 --> 00:47:30,721
whether it 's crackling electrics or bells or the sounds of the laboratory ,

931
00:47:30,806 --> 00:47:32,843
the sounds of merrymaking is a very ...

932
00:47:32,933 --> 00:47:37,097
You know , this is only a couple of years after sound properly came in ,

933
00:47:37,188 --> 00:47:42,183
and James Whale is often accused of being a very theatrical , static kind of director ,

934
00:47:42,276 --> 00:47:44,361
and I think these sequences give the lie to that .

935
00:47:44,445 --> 00:47:49,235
A long , long tracking shot down the village to show off the great set that Universal had ,

936
00:47:49,325 --> 00:47:51,075
with all the merrymaking villagers .

937
00:47:51,160 --> 00:47:53,401
Are we in the Tirol ? Are we in Switzerland ?

938
00:47:53,496 --> 00:47:53,662
Are we in Bavaria ,

939
00:47:53,746 --> 00:47:54,486
Are we in Bavaria ,

940
00:47:54,580 --> 00:47:57,332
which is where Carl Laemmle , the head of the studio , came from ?

941
00:47:57,416 --> 00:48:00,408
I don 't know . It 's Ruritania . It 's never-never land .

942
00:48:00,503 --> 00:48:03,630
And now we 're by the side of the lake . It was filmed at Malibou Lake ,

943
00:48:03,714 --> 00:48:06,877
a rare location moment in this film ,

944
00:48:06,967 --> 00:48:09,174
where Little Maria is playing .

945
00:48:09,261 --> 00:48:12,720
She 's played by the seven-year-old Marilyn Harris .

946
00:48:12,807 --> 00:48:15,970
And this sequence was shot on September 28 , 29 , 1931 .

947
00:48:16,060 --> 00:48:17,971
The film was shot largely in sequence ,

948
00:48:18,062 --> 00:48:20,063
so we 'd been shooting since the end of August .

949
00:48:20,147 --> 00:48:21,523
They had been filming for a month .

950
00:48:21,607 --> 00:48:23,525
And this is the famous scene by the lake .

951
00:48:23,609 --> 00:48:29,446
In the film , the father , Ludwig , goes off and leaves the little girl to play by herself .

952
00:48:29,532 --> 00:48:33,617
In the shooting script , interestingly , it 's her mother who leaves her to play ,

953
00:48:33,702 --> 00:48:36,069
a buxom peasant woman in her early 30s ,

954
00:48:36,163 --> 00:48:40,292
who goes into the kitchen of the cottage and leaves her child playing by the lakeside .

955
00:48:40,376 --> 00:48:44,210
So she could conceivably have seen what happens now ,

956
00:48:44,296 --> 00:48:47,163
which is how everyone knows that it 's a murder later on .

957
00:48:47,258 --> 00:48:49,593
In the absence of anyone actually seeing what happened ,

958
00:48:49,677 --> 00:48:52,886
we don 't quite know why they immediately jump to the conclusion

959
00:48:52,972 --> 00:48:54,133
that it 's a murder .

960
00:48:54,223 --> 00:48:56,430
This very , very touching scene

961
00:48:56,517 --> 00:48:58,178
that stimulated , for example ,

962
00:48:58,269 --> 00:49:02,012
inspired a Spanish film of 1973 , The Spirit of the Beehive ,

963
00:49:02,106 --> 00:49:03,267
Víctor Erice ,

964
00:49:03,357 --> 00:49:06,691
which is all about a little girl who grows up in Franco 's Spain ,

965
00:49:06,777 --> 00:49:08,487
and for whom this sequence means so much .

966
00:49:08,571 --> 00:49:11,563
It helps her understand adult life ,

967
00:49:11,657 --> 00:49:14,945
it helps her to navigate the difficulties of grown-up life .

968
00:49:15,035 --> 00:49:17,242
So it had a really touching afterlife .

969
00:49:17,872 --> 00:49:20,159
And this was a slightly controversial scene

970
00:49:20,249 --> 00:49:24,117
in that Karloff wasn 't sure that James Whale was playing it the right way .

971
00:49:24,211 --> 00:49:28,170
In the shooting script , he places the little girl on the surface of the water ,

972
00:49:28,257 --> 00:49:30,294
an expectant smile on his face .

973
00:49:30,384 --> 00:49:34,179
" The flowers float , they 're little boats , so maybe the little girl will float as well . "

974
00:49:34,263 --> 00:49:36,630
So he places heron the surface of the water ,

975
00:49:36,724 --> 00:49:40,934
a reference to the scene where little William Frankenstein is killed in the novel ,

976
00:49:41,020 --> 00:49:42,636
Frankenstein 's younger brother ,

977
00:49:42,730 --> 00:49:46,098
and where his disabled sister is thrown in the lake

978
00:49:46,192 --> 00:49:49,184
in Peggy Webling 's version , off-stage .

979
00:49:49,278 --> 00:49:52,030
Here , it 's a little girl who has no connection with the family ,

980
00:49:52,114 --> 00:49:56,529
but who doesn 't have any problem playing games with the creature .

981
00:49:59,872 --> 00:50:01,158
See how mine floats ?

982
00:50:03,000 --> 00:50:05,043
FRAYLING : Very , very touching moment by Karloff .

983
00:50:05,127 --> 00:50:07,212
This is the first person who 's been nice to him in his life ,

984
00:50:07,296 --> 00:50:09,089
and he just doesn 't know how to behave .

985
00:50:09,173 --> 00:50:12,507
But sheer excitement , like a child , as he throws the flowers in .

986
00:50:12,593 --> 00:50:18,430
But then he picks up the child and chucks her into the lake .

987
00:50:18,516 --> 00:50:20,016
And Karloff thought that the original version ,

988
00:50:20,100 --> 00:50:23,343
which is placing her gently on the surface of the lake ,

989
00:50:23,437 --> 00:50:25,021
would 've been slightly less gruesome .

990
00:50:25,105 --> 00:50:26,523
He actually hurls her into the lake .

991
00:50:26,607 --> 00:50:30,861
And there was originally a shot , a close-up , of her struggling in the water and drowning .

992
00:50:30,945 --> 00:50:34,364
So with the best intentions in the world , he 's destroyed the thing he loves .

993
00:50:34,448 --> 00:50:36,359
And off he goes into the woods ,

994
00:50:36,450 --> 00:50:39,784
completely confused by the one character who 's been nice to him ,

995
00:50:39,870 --> 00:50:42,612
and he 's destroyed it , unwittingly .

996
00:50:42,706 --> 00:50:46,791
" But Whale said , " We must have him actually throwing the child in the lake .

997
00:50:46,877 --> 00:50:49,039
" You see , it 's all part of the ritual . "

998
00:50:49,129 --> 00:50:51,089
That you have to have a moment like this

999
00:50:51,173 --> 00:50:54,086
for the final sequences actually to make sense .

1000
00:50:54,176 --> 00:50:56,508
[ CHEERFUL MUSIC PLAYING ]

1001
00:50:58,764 --> 00:51:01,349
And then we cut back to the All Quiet on the Western Front village ,

1002
00:51:01,433 --> 00:51:04,016
where the lederhosen and the merry-go-rounds

1003
00:51:04,103 --> 00:51:06,354
and the excitement of the wedding are in full swing .

1004
00:51:06,438 --> 00:51:08,398
Some people reckon these sequences go on a bit long ,

1005
00:51:08,482 --> 00:51:10,894
they 're showing off about the production values

1006
00:51:10,985 --> 00:51:13,737
of having a few extras all dressed up in Tyrolean costumes .

1007
00:51:13,821 --> 00:51:16,948
And maybe James Whale overdoes it , but the reason for this is ...

1008
00:51:17,032 --> 00:51:20,577
It 's like in a Western , where you have the merrymaking or the saloon scene ,

1009
00:51:20,661 --> 00:51:23,073
and someone walks in and spoils the party .

1010
00:51:23,163 --> 00:51:24,494
" The Indians are coming ! "

1011
00:51:24,582 --> 00:51:26,664
Well , in this case , it 's Little Maria .

1012
00:51:26,792 --> 00:51:31,127
And in the shooting script , it 's the mother who carried the child up the street .

1013
00:51:31,213 --> 00:51:34,001
In the film , it 's the father , Ludwig .

1014
00:51:34,091 --> 00:51:36,958
When the Little Maria sequence was pruned ,

1015
00:51:37,052 --> 00:51:40,795
in some places , on first release , in many places , in 1937 ,

1016
00:51:40,889 --> 00:51:45,178
it finished with the Karloff character leaning forward in this expectant way ,

1017
00:51:45,269 --> 00:51:49,105
and cut to the next time we see her , being carried up the street by her father .

1018
00:51:49,189 --> 00:51:52,853
It actually created the impression that the child had been molested ,

1019
00:51:52,943 --> 00:51:54,361
the child had been raped in some way ,

1020
00:51:54,445 --> 00:51:55,776
and had been murdered .

1021
00:51:55,863 --> 00:51:59,282
It 's an extraordinary example of how censorship can actually make things worse .

1022
00:51:59,366 --> 00:52:01,660
Because when the sequence is put back together again ,

1023
00:52:01,744 --> 00:52:03,451
it 's actually rather touching .

1024
00:52:07,708 --> 00:52:08,698
Well , what is it ?

1025
00:52:08,792 --> 00:52:11,127
FRAYLING : And then , after that darkness , into the light .

1026
00:52:11,211 --> 00:52:12,372
The day of the wedding ,

1027
00:52:12,463 --> 00:52:16,923
with this astonishing bridal train and bridal gown for Mae Clarke .

1028
00:52:17,009 --> 00:52:19,469
In the shooting script , this takes place in the bedroom .

1029
00:52:19,553 --> 00:52:22,472
It isn 't clear whether this is an adjunct to the living room or the bedroom .

1030
00:52:22,556 --> 00:52:23,515
It 's a strange place to be

1031
00:52:23,599 --> 00:52:26,637
on wedding morning in a traditional baronial household .

1032
00:52:26,727 --> 00:52:29,094
But she has a feeling that something is wrong .

1033
00:52:29,188 --> 00:52:31,189
Where 's Dr. Waldman ? Why is he late for the wedding ?

1034
00:52:31,273 --> 00:52:32,855
Oh , he 's always late .

1035
00:52:32,941 --> 00:52:35,819
FRAYLING : Victor comes in , in the shooting script , and says ,

1036
00:52:35,903 --> 00:52:38,613
" Waldman has been murdered . They found him in the watchtower . "

1037
00:52:38,697 --> 00:52:41,280
" It 's loose now . People have seen him in the hills . "

1038
00:52:41,367 --> 00:52:43,859
" Shepherds are found dead next to their flocks . "

1039
00:52:43,952 --> 00:52:48,071
Lots of explanation of the creature having got away from the watchtower

1040
00:52:48,165 --> 00:52:50,327
and rampaging around the countryside .

1041
00:52:50,417 --> 00:52:53,079
It 's very , very cryptic in the film version .

1042
00:52:53,170 --> 00:52:56,631
I think Whale felt that it slowed things down , so he cuts Victor 's dialogue ,

1043
00:52:56,715 --> 00:52:59,342
and as a result , we don 't actually quite know what goes on .

1044
00:52:59,426 --> 00:53:00,594
Also in the shooting script ,

1045
00:53:00,678 --> 00:53:04,137
Frankenstein puts on her head the orange blossom wreath ,

1046
00:53:04,223 --> 00:53:07,136
which he 's been given by the Baron , still in his hand

1047
00:53:07,226 --> 00:53:10,186
because , actually , this scene was continuous from the wedding scene ,

1048
00:53:10,270 --> 00:53:13,729
and the Little Maria scene came later in the shooting script .

1049
00:53:13,816 --> 00:53:14,941
It 's still in his hand ,

1050
00:53:15,025 --> 00:53:18,438
and he places the orange blossom wreath on her head to reassure her ,

1051
00:53:18,529 --> 00:53:22,657
so that when she 's attacked by the creature , she has the orange blossom on her head .

1052
00:53:22,741 --> 00:53:24,075
Which is rather a nice touch .

1053
00:53:24,159 --> 00:53:25,309
How beautiful you look .

1054
00:53:25,994 --> 00:53:28,577
- VICTOR : Henry ! Henry ! - [ KNOCKING ON DOOR ]

1055
00:53:28,664 --> 00:53:29,699
What 's that ?

1056
00:53:29,790 --> 00:53:32,190
FRAYLING : Here 's Victor , coming in with his news .

1057
00:53:32,292 --> 00:53:34,909
- Dr . Waldman ! - What about Dr. Waldman ?

1058
00:53:35,379 --> 00:53:38,747
- Henry , don 't leave me . Don 't leave me . - No , darling , you stay here .

1059
00:53:39,133 --> 00:53:41,215
Henry . Henry !

1060
00:53:41,552 --> 00:53:43,094
FRAYLING : Why does he lock the door ?

1061
00:53:43,178 --> 00:53:44,929
He locked the door of the laboratory

1062
00:53:45,013 --> 00:53:48,381
to make sure he wasn 't interrupted during the operation scene ,

1063
00:53:48,475 --> 00:53:51,061
and he 's heard about Dr. Waldman , and he locks the door .

1064
00:53:51,145 --> 00:53:54,604
It 's a sort of paranoid reflex that he should be secret .

1065
00:53:54,690 --> 00:53:57,690
Of course , it actually causes all sorts of problems later on .

1066
00:53:57,776 --> 00:54:00,814
And here we have a rather farfetched set of moments ,

1067
00:54:00,946 --> 00:54:03,531
where the creature appears to be something of a ventriloquist .

1068
00:54:03,615 --> 00:54:05,450
" It 's upstairs ! " So they look upstairs .

1069
00:54:05,534 --> 00:54:07,911
" It 's in the cellar ! " So they look in the cellar .

1070
00:54:07,995 --> 00:54:11,579
" It 's Elizabeth ! " So they go back to the living room / bedroom .

1071
00:54:11,665 --> 00:54:13,708
Either the creature is very good at throwing his voice

1072
00:54:13,792 --> 00:54:15,960
or they can 't quite tell where the sound is coming from .

1073
00:54:16,044 --> 00:54:18,206
But it 's an excuse for James Whale 's camera

1074
00:54:18,297 --> 00:54:20,584
to wander round the Frankenstein household

1075
00:54:20,674 --> 00:54:24,508
and go through walls in a way that he rather enjoyed doing .

1076
00:54:25,053 --> 00:54:29,923
Now , this sequence is taken directly from The Cabinet of Dr. Caligari

1077
00:54:30,017 --> 00:54:32,509
by Robert Wiene , 1919 ,

1078
00:54:32,603 --> 00:54:37,268
where the somnambulist , Cesare , is standing at the window ,

1079
00:54:37,357 --> 00:54:41,567
comes in , and the girl , played by Lil Dagover , is asleep .

1080
00:54:41,653 --> 00:54:44,441
And it became a classic of expressionist cinema ,

1081
00:54:44,531 --> 00:54:46,783
the woman under threat , the woman in the bridal gown ,

1082
00:54:46,867 --> 00:54:50,485
the woman in her chemise , the woman in her nightclothes ,

1083
00:54:50,579 --> 00:54:54,117
and the threat , coming in through the window .

1084
00:54:54,208 --> 00:54:55,792
And it 's a silent film moment , this ,

1085
00:54:55,876 --> 00:54:58,664
because in his huge asphalt spreader 's boots ,

1086
00:54:58,754 --> 00:55:00,296
she 'd have heard the creature behind her .

1087
00:55:00,380 --> 00:55:03,380
But she doesn 't , and so the scream is a kind of delayed drop .

1088
00:55:03,467 --> 00:55:04,467
When they were filming this sequence ,

1089
00:55:04,551 --> 00:55:07,011
which is only cryptically mentioned in the shooting script ,

1090
00:55:07,095 --> 00:55:08,471
James Whale said to Mae Clarke ,

1091
00:55:08,555 --> 00:55:11,141
" Now you 've all read the script . Let 's do the mechanics . "

1092
00:55:11,225 --> 00:55:15,093
And he made the scene much more elaborate in the actual acting of it .

1093
00:55:15,187 --> 00:55:18,305
The slow burn of the creature stepping forward ,

1094
00:55:18,398 --> 00:55:22,141
and then a shot taken directly from a famous painting

1095
00:55:22,236 --> 00:55:25,399
by Johann Heinrich Füssli , John Henry Fuseli ,

1096
00:55:25,489 --> 00:55:29,153
called The Nightmare , of the girl draped over the bed ,

1097
00:55:29,243 --> 00:55:32,952
in the case of the painting , with the nightmare perched upon her chest .

1098
00:55:33,038 --> 00:55:34,289
And it 's taken directly from it ,

1099
00:55:34,373 --> 00:55:36,865
and it was used in the advertising for the film ,

1100
00:55:36,959 --> 00:55:40,668
in the posters , the tie-in book , this image from The Nightmare .

1101
00:55:40,754 --> 00:55:43,715
In the shooting script , you see that moment from the door frame .

1102
00:55:43,799 --> 00:55:45,884
You get a completely different angle on it .

1103
00:55:45,968 --> 00:55:49,053
And also in the shooting script , Elizabeth is lying across the bed ,

1104
00:55:49,137 --> 00:55:52,432
apparently dead , her wedding gown is in tatters , and there 's a sense ,

1105
00:55:52,516 --> 00:55:54,517
she doesn 't say anything , she just falls back ,

1106
00:55:54,601 --> 00:55:56,888
that she has been killed , as in the novel .

1107
00:55:56,979 --> 00:56:01,189
The creature has actually killed Elizabeth , Frankenstein 's fiancée .

1108
00:56:01,275 --> 00:56:05,564
But it 's left ambiguous , and of course we 'll learn later that she survived .

1109
00:56:05,654 --> 00:56:08,490
But it 's an interesting use of a northern European painting

1110
00:56:08,574 --> 00:56:10,190
to sort out a visual problem

1111
00:56:10,450 --> 00:56:14,239
and create this image of the woman draped over the bed .

1112
00:56:14,329 --> 00:56:16,366
A lot of northern European émigrés

1113
00:56:16,456 --> 00:56:19,209
came to Hollywood as production designers and art directors

1114
00:56:19,293 --> 00:56:20,752
and brought that aesthetic with them .

1115
00:56:20,836 --> 00:56:22,827
German expressionism , German symbolism ,

1116
00:56:22,921 --> 00:56:25,583
19th-century German painting , Romantic painting .

1117
00:56:25,674 --> 00:56:28,968
And you see lots and lots of references , particularly in horror movies ,

1118
00:56:29,052 --> 00:56:31,168
to that whole European tradition of art ,

1119
00:56:31,263 --> 00:56:33,174
and Whale was consciously using it .

1120
00:56:33,265 --> 00:56:35,097
[ CHURCH BELLS TOLLING ]

1121
00:56:35,183 --> 00:56:37,424
[ PEOPLE SPEAKING INDISTINCTLY ]

1122
00:56:42,941 --> 00:56:44,477
[ ALL CLAMORING ]

1123
00:56:49,489 --> 00:56:51,105
And now we have the merrymaking

1124
00:56:51,199 --> 00:56:56,535
interrupted by Little Maria and her father going to see the Burgomaster .

1125
00:56:57,080 --> 00:56:59,162
Not little William , the brother ,

1126
00:56:59,249 --> 00:57:02,833
not little Katrina , the sister from the Peggy Webling play ,

1127
00:57:02,920 --> 00:57:04,661
but Little Maria and the father .

1128
00:57:05,172 --> 00:57:08,132
And they 're going to the Burgomaster because they are convinced that it 's murder .

1129
00:57:08,216 --> 00:57:10,708
It 's a public issue . It 's not just an accident .

1130
00:57:10,802 --> 00:57:12,178
Again , in the shooting script ,

1131
00:57:12,262 --> 00:57:14,681
the mother could have seen that it wasn 't an accident ,

1132
00:57:14,765 --> 00:57:17,507
but this is now an assumption on the part of the mob ,

1133
00:57:17,601 --> 00:57:20,343
and the mob begins to become , more and more ,

1134
00:57:20,437 --> 00:57:22,019
a character in the play now .

1135
00:57:22,105 --> 00:57:24,524
A drunken , raging mob . They 've had too much to drink .

1136
00:57:24,608 --> 00:57:25,848
The Baron predicted

1137
00:57:25,943 --> 00:57:29,237
that when they started having a hangover , they 'd start fighting each other .

1138
00:57:29,321 --> 00:57:32,439
And instead , they turn into this rather ugly , raging mob

1139
00:57:32,532 --> 00:57:36,070
that acts like a single unit for the rest of the film ,

1140
00:57:36,161 --> 00:57:40,325
which was much parodied later on in Young Frankenstein by Mel Brooks .

1141
00:57:40,415 --> 00:57:42,292
It 's no longer flaming torches and stones ,

1142
00:57:42,376 --> 00:57:45,378
it 's rotten tomatoes [ CHUCKLES ] that get thrown at the creature .

1143
00:57:45,462 --> 00:57:48,079
But this idea of the mob as a character in the film

1144
00:57:48,173 --> 00:57:49,716
really dominates the last part .

1145
00:57:49,800 --> 00:57:52,758
And here 's Frankenstein in his jodhpurs and sports jacket ,

1146
00:57:52,844 --> 00:57:54,304
no longer in his wedding outfit ,

1147
00:57:54,388 --> 00:57:58,882
suggesting that Victor , if all goes wrong , Victor should take over ,

1148
00:57:58,976 --> 00:58:01,764
and , indeed , he might marry Elizabeth .

1149
00:58:01,853 --> 00:58:03,218
So Elizabeth survived .

1150
00:58:03,313 --> 00:58:05,940
Maybe this is a hangover from the John Balderston version ,

1151
00:58:06,024 --> 00:58:08,982
where Victor was the ex-fiancé of Elizabeth .

1152
00:58:09,069 --> 00:58:11,936
In fact , there are various hangovers from earlier scripts

1153
00:58:12,030 --> 00:58:13,656
that don 't quite work in the film .

1154
00:58:13,740 --> 00:58:15,440
Earlier on , the Baron has referred

1155
00:58:15,534 --> 00:58:16,326
to Frankenstein 's experiments " in the old mill . "

1156
00:58:16,410 --> 00:58:18,536
To Frankenstein 's experiments " in the old mill . "

1157
00:58:18,620 --> 00:58:21,831
And they were , indeed , taking place in the old mill in the Robert Florey version ,

1158
00:58:21,915 --> 00:58:24,167
but in this version , they 're taking place in the watchtower

1159
00:58:24,251 --> 00:58:26,788
and somehow , no one noticed that continuity error .

1160
00:58:26,878 --> 00:58:29,916
So , in some senses , the final version bears the stamp

1161
00:58:30,007 --> 00:58:32,965
of the myriad of interpretations of Frankenstein

1162
00:58:33,051 --> 00:58:36,089
which had happened in order to turn it into a movie .

1163
00:58:36,179 --> 00:58:40,764
As late as early 1931 , in the studio , someone wrote a memo

1164
00:58:40,851 --> 00:58:45,015
saying they didn 't think that this was an adaptable property .

1165
00:58:45,105 --> 00:58:46,345
It was too complicated .

1166
00:58:46,440 --> 00:58:49,649
The novel consisted of memoirs and letters

1167
00:58:49,776 --> 00:58:51,569
and the autobiography of the creature ,

1168
00:58:51,653 --> 00:58:54,691
and it was three volumes , and lots and lots of disquisitions

1169
00:58:54,781 --> 00:58:59,992
on all the social , political , economic , scientific , psychological theories

1170
00:59:00,078 --> 00:59:01,371
that Mary Shelley was interested in .

1171
00:59:01,455 --> 00:59:05,915
How could you adapt that into a crisp , short horror movie ?

1172
00:59:06,001 --> 00:59:09,039
Well , we 've seen various ways in which shortcuts were taken .

1173
00:59:09,129 --> 00:59:13,464
The brain , the laboratory sequence , the creature and its makeup .

1174
00:59:13,550 --> 00:59:16,542
And in that process , some loose trails were left .

1175
00:59:16,636 --> 00:59:18,126
[ DOGS BARKING ]

1176
00:59:18,221 --> 00:59:19,882
[ PEOPLE CLAMORING ]

1177
00:59:24,061 --> 00:59:25,979
Here , a very gendered approach to the village .

1178
00:59:26,063 --> 00:59:27,355
The women are at the windows ,

1179
00:59:27,439 --> 00:59:31,139
the women are standing at the edge of the main street , crossing themselves ,

1180
00:59:31,485 --> 00:59:35,103
while the men go out hunting in the woods , in the mountains , in the lake ,

1181
00:59:35,197 --> 00:59:38,658
in the various sectors that they 've been apportioned by the Burgomaster .

1182
00:59:38,742 --> 00:59:40,107
Frankenstein has said ,

1183
00:59:40,202 --> 00:59:44,036
" I made him with these hands , and with these hands , I will destroy him . "

1184
00:59:44,247 --> 00:59:45,999
And there 's much emphasis in the novel , as well ,

1185
00:59:46,083 --> 00:59:49,621
of how Frankenstein created the creature with his hands .

1186
00:59:49,711 --> 00:59:53,548
And recent criticism has suggested this isn 't just a reference to craftsmanship ,

1187
00:59:53,632 --> 00:59:55,088
a handmade creature ,

1188
00:59:55,175 --> 00:59:57,837
but also , it might be a reference to masturbation .

1189
00:59:58,011 --> 01:00:01,925
That , actually , with these hands , Frankenstein has been a semen donor

1190
01:00:02,015 --> 01:00:04,507
in order to create this new life on the slab .

1191
01:00:04,851 --> 01:00:07,809
If so , I 'm not sure that the Production Code

1192
01:00:07,896 --> 01:00:10,440
would later have allowed the line " I made him with these hands "

1193
01:00:10,524 --> 01:00:11,514
to get through .

1194
01:00:11,608 --> 01:00:13,690
But maybe that 's reading into it .

1195
01:00:14,486 --> 01:00:15,476
This way !

1196
01:00:15,570 --> 01:00:17,026
Come on , men !

1197
01:00:17,114 --> 01:00:19,196
[ ALL CLAMORING ]

1198
01:00:19,282 --> 01:00:23,526
FRAYLING : So now we 're actually building up to the climax

1199
01:00:23,620 --> 01:00:27,454
with the flaming torches and the villagers looking for the creature .

1200
01:00:27,541 --> 01:00:30,334
In the shooting script , there 's much more emphasis on the Baron at this point .

1201
01:00:30,418 --> 01:00:32,503
The Baron , who gets lost in the crowd and keeps saying ,

1202
01:00:32,587 --> 01:00:36,171
" is my son among you ? Where 's my son ? Have you seen my son ? "

1203
01:00:36,675 --> 01:00:39,135
And towards the end , when they want to burn the mill ,

1204
01:00:39,219 --> 01:00:41,387
he says , " No , you mustn 't do that . You must save my son . "

1205
01:00:41,471 --> 01:00:43,973
And there 's a touching feeling that the relationship between them ,

1206
01:00:44,057 --> 01:00:45,475
which started off so negatively ,

1207
01:00:45,559 --> 01:00:47,643
" My father never believed anything about me , "

1208
01:00:47,727 --> 01:00:51,345
actually is beginning to get closer as a result of this crisis .

1209
01:00:51,439 --> 01:00:53,357
But that 's all gone in the James Whale version .

1210
01:00:53,441 --> 01:00:56,110
We just have Frankenstein saying , " My father never understands me . "

1211
01:00:56,194 --> 01:00:58,654
And really , he disappears from the plot at this point .

1212
01:00:58,738 --> 01:01:00,194
The rest , come with me .

1213
01:01:00,282 --> 01:01:02,193
[ DOGS BARKING ]

1214
01:01:02,284 --> 01:01:05,578
So here we are amongst the studio rocks , rather like the ones we began with ,

1215
01:01:05,662 --> 01:01:08,154
with the gibbet and the discovery of the corpse

1216
01:01:08,248 --> 01:01:10,785
against an angry sky , a lowering sky ,

1217
01:01:10,876 --> 01:01:13,726
rather a crinkled backdrop , actually , if you look closely ,

1218
01:01:14,129 --> 01:01:16,798
And this is the equivalent of the Chamonix scene in the novel ,

1219
01:01:16,882 --> 01:01:19,374
where the creature finally meets his maker .

1220
01:01:19,926 --> 01:01:24,341
And instead of a long discussion about ethics and the meaning of life ,

1221
01:01:24,431 --> 01:01:27,389
and will Frankenstein create a mate for him ,

1222
01:01:27,475 --> 01:01:31,764
we have the creature getting his revenge at last .

1223
01:01:32,939 --> 01:01:35,522
This injured villager lying in the hills

1224
01:01:35,609 --> 01:01:37,896
was added after the shooting script stage ,

1225
01:01:37,986 --> 01:01:41,114
and is probably a visualization of the line from Victor earlier ,

1226
01:01:41,198 --> 01:01:44,611
about shepherds being hurt and killed out in the fields ,

1227
01:01:44,701 --> 01:01:46,283
only they leave it till now .

1228
01:01:46,369 --> 01:01:48,704
And also that it can direct people in two directions ,

1229
01:01:48,788 --> 01:01:52,531
so that Frankenstein can be isolated when he meets the creature .

1230
01:01:52,626 --> 01:01:56,629
In the script , there 's lots of reference to how ugly the mob has become at this stage .

1231
01:01:56,713 --> 01:02:00,377
Their angry , sweating faces , the baying of the hounds .

1232
01:02:00,467 --> 01:02:01,884
What scent are the hounds after ?

1233
01:02:01,968 --> 01:02:05,381
In the script , a piece of the creature 's jacket is found ,

1234
01:02:05,472 --> 01:02:08,141
and it 's shown to the hounds , and that explains why they 're on the scent .

1235
01:02:08,225 --> 01:02:10,059
We don 't quite know why they 're on the scent in this version .

1236
01:02:10,143 --> 01:02:13,977
But you 've got the baying of the hounds , the angry mob , the flaming torches ,

1237
01:02:14,064 --> 01:02:15,731
and there 's a sense of , you know ...

1238
01:02:15,815 --> 01:02:18,276
Another aspect of the sympathy we feel for the creature

1239
01:02:18,360 --> 01:02:23,321
is that this is kind of mass behavior of a fairly mindless kind .

1240
01:02:23,657 --> 01:02:25,819
They 're just out to kill the bogeyman .

1241
01:02:25,909 --> 01:02:28,116
Herr Frankenstein ! Where are you ?

1242
01:02:28,203 --> 01:02:30,410
[ DOGS HOWLING ]

1243
01:02:30,497 --> 01:02:32,415
- I think he 's up there . Come on , follow him . - Follow him .

1244
01:02:32,499 --> 01:02:35,958
FRAYLING : By now , the filming was at the very end of September ,

1245
01:02:36,044 --> 01:02:37,500
beginning of October , 1931 .

1246
01:02:37,587 --> 01:02:39,954
They 'd started on August the 24th .

1247
01:02:40,048 --> 01:02:44,508
They were a couple of days over schedule and a little bit over budget , but not much .

1248
01:02:44,844 --> 01:02:48,264
And having shot in sequence , they were building up to the final sequences

1249
01:02:48,348 --> 01:02:49,338
set in the mill .

1250
01:02:49,432 --> 01:02:54,017
The reason they were set in a mill was from Robert Florey 's outline .

1251
01:02:54,646 --> 01:02:57,064
He reckoned that the operation should take place in an old mill ,

1252
01:02:57,148 --> 01:02:58,107
but so should the climax .

1253
01:02:58,191 --> 01:03:00,568
And the creature would make his way back to the old mill ,

1254
01:03:00,652 --> 01:03:02,486
because that 's where his origins came from .

1255
01:03:02,570 --> 01:03:05,779
It was finding his way home , finding his way back into the womb ,

1256
01:03:05,865 --> 01:03:08,027
and this rickety old mill was the womb .

1257
01:03:08,201 --> 01:03:11,954
The reason he chose a mill , Florey said , was that he had an apartment in Los Angeles

1258
01:03:12,038 --> 01:03:15,030
which overlooked a branch of Van de Kamp Bakery ,

1259
01:03:15,125 --> 01:03:17,366
which had a mill as its corporate image .

1260
01:03:17,460 --> 01:03:19,670
And while he was doing the outline , it gave him the idea .

1261
01:03:19,754 --> 01:03:20,755
That may or may not be so .

1262
01:03:20,839 --> 01:03:25,458
Actually , there 's some early versions of the Frankenstein play in the 1820s and ' 30s

1263
01:03:25,552 --> 01:03:28,095
where the creature is killed in all sorts of outlandish ways .

1264
01:03:28,179 --> 01:03:30,341
In an avalanche , falling over a cliff ,

1265
01:03:30,432 --> 01:03:32,725
and in one of them , he dies in an old , burning mill .

1266
01:03:32,809 --> 01:03:34,977
So it 's not quite clear where that came from .

1267
01:03:35,061 --> 01:03:37,393
This way . Come on . Hurry . Hurry !

1268
01:03:37,480 --> 01:03:39,562
[ ALL SHOUTING INDISTINCTLY ]

1269
01:03:39,649 --> 01:03:41,060
[ DOGS BARKING ]

1270
01:03:41,151 --> 01:03:43,438
There ! Turn the hounds loose !

1271
01:03:45,864 --> 01:03:47,156
FRAYLING : The famous moment where the creature

1272
01:03:47,240 --> 01:03:50,232
carries his creator away over his shoulder

1273
01:03:50,452 --> 01:03:54,912
and Karloff complained that Colin Clive was actually pretty heavy ,

1274
01:03:54,998 --> 01:03:58,332
and it caused all sorts of back problems for him later in life .

1275
01:03:58,418 --> 01:04:01,285
The mill is exactly like the production drawing

1276
01:04:01,379 --> 01:04:05,043
by Herman Rosse under the guidance of Danny Hall .

1277
01:04:05,133 --> 01:04:09,673
A great expressionist mill , with tattered sails hanging in shreds ,

1278
01:04:09,763 --> 01:04:12,972
and the wood 's falling off it . It 's a decaying flour mill .

1279
01:04:13,058 --> 01:04:16,676
And a confined space forgoing up , up , up .

1280
01:04:16,770 --> 01:04:20,809
The creature is going up to the light , to the sky , where he came from .

1281
01:04:20,899 --> 01:04:23,317
I mean , why is he taking Frankenstein out to the mill ?

1282
01:04:23,401 --> 01:04:25,736
Does he simply want to murder him in front of the peasants ,

1283
01:04:25,820 --> 01:04:28,027
or is he making for the heavens ?

1284
01:04:30,283 --> 01:04:31,694
MAN : Once more .

1285
01:04:31,785 --> 01:04:33,116
[ GRUNTING ]

1286
01:04:35,455 --> 01:04:36,786
MAN : Once more .

1287
01:04:36,998 --> 01:04:39,239
FRAYLING : The scene of the gear going round ,

1288
01:04:39,334 --> 01:04:41,168
and seeing the faces of the two protagonists

1289
01:04:41,252 --> 01:04:44,495
actually framed by the wooden gearing mechanism

1290
01:04:44,589 --> 01:04:47,047
was added by James Whale on the set .

1291
01:04:47,133 --> 01:04:49,750
So like a lot of the more visual moments in the film ,

1292
01:04:50,053 --> 01:04:52,303
it was actually quite a last-minute decision ,

1293
01:04:52,389 --> 01:04:55,177
in order to make the film more exciting visually ,

1294
01:04:55,266 --> 01:04:56,882
and give it a bit of style .

1295
01:04:56,976 --> 01:04:58,853
It 's a particularly expressionist moment ,

1296
01:04:58,937 --> 01:05:03,272
where expressionist films often framed the image through arches , through windows ,

1297
01:05:03,358 --> 01:05:07,727
in order to create unusual shapes to the screen for the viewer to watch them .

1298
01:05:07,821 --> 01:05:10,404
Well , this was framed through the gearing mechanism .

1299
01:05:10,490 --> 01:05:14,108
And it creates an unusual shape to frame the two protagonists

1300
01:05:14,202 --> 01:05:16,364
for this climactic confrontation .

1301
01:05:16,454 --> 01:05:19,446
Now , what happens now was very muddled in the film ,

1302
01:05:19,541 --> 01:05:21,908
and no one was quite sure what to do .

1303
01:05:22,001 --> 01:05:23,241
Frankenstein , in ...

1304
01:05:23,336 --> 01:05:25,921
It 's clear from the shooting script that Frankenstein gets killed .

1305
01:05:26,005 --> 01:05:27,005
" His lifeless body . "

1306
01:05:27,090 --> 01:05:30,628
The same phrase that 's applied to Elizabeth , " Her lifeless body . "

1307
01:05:30,718 --> 01:05:32,925
So , as in the novel , they both die .

1308
01:05:33,012 --> 01:05:35,412
In the case of the novel , it 's at the North Pole ,

1309
01:05:35,807 --> 01:05:38,642
and then the creature goes off on an ice floe into the distance .

1310
01:05:38,726 --> 01:05:40,436
But clearly , either they wanted a sequel

1311
01:05:40,520 --> 01:05:42,188
or they thought it was too unhappy an ending ,

1312
01:05:42,272 --> 01:05:45,358
although Colin Clive thought it was one of the strengths , actually , of the film ,

1313
01:05:45,442 --> 01:05:47,276
that it dared to have an unhappy ending .

1314
01:05:47,360 --> 01:05:50,460
They decided to make Frankenstein survive , and added the moment

1315
01:05:50,572 --> 01:05:53,908
where he 's thrown over the parapet and lands on the sails of the windmill ,

1316
01:05:53,992 --> 01:05:56,660
which isn 't mentioned in the shooting script , which breaks his fall .

1317
01:05:56,744 --> 01:05:56,952
And then he lands on the ground and he 's okay .

1318
01:05:57,036 --> 01:05:59,494
And then he lands on the ground and he 's okay .

1319
01:05:59,581 --> 01:06:02,164
And an epilogue is added

1320
01:06:02,250 --> 01:06:05,709
where the Baron back-announces the joke

1321
01:06:06,004 --> 01:06:09,338
about the grandmother not letting the wine be drunk

1322
01:06:09,424 --> 01:06:12,633
and the maids reappear , the ones who drank the champagne earlier ,

1323
01:06:12,719 --> 01:06:14,720
and it 's all light , and all is sweetness and light

1324
01:06:14,804 --> 01:06:17,098
after the darkness of the holocaust of the mill .

1325
01:06:17,182 --> 01:06:19,308
In fact , it 's called a " holocaust " in the script .

1326
01:06:19,392 --> 01:06:24,262
As the fire consumes the mill , the mill is a solid sheet of flame .

1327
01:06:24,355 --> 01:06:27,817
And in the shooting script , the film ends with a long shot of the mill burning .

1328
01:06:27,901 --> 01:06:31,565
But then you have this cut to this epilogue that was added subsequently .

1329
01:06:31,905 --> 01:06:35,199
Colin Clive was no longer around , so there 's a stand-in in the epilogue ,

1330
01:06:35,283 --> 01:06:38,651
just glimpsed through the open door , but it 's mainly the Baron .

1331
01:06:38,745 --> 01:06:40,746
And by that stage , they must have realized

1332
01:06:40,830 --> 01:06:44,664
that they had a great piece of myth-making in this film .

1333
01:06:44,751 --> 01:06:48,337
But what they couldn 't have known is that it would take over from the novel .

1334
01:06:48,421 --> 01:06:51,382
Everyone knows what the creature looks like . They call him " The Monster . "

1335
01:06:51,466 --> 01:06:54,927
Everyone knows the story of Frankenstein . Very , very few people have read the novel .

1336
01:06:55,011 --> 01:06:57,503
It actually took over from the literary experience

1337
01:06:57,597 --> 01:06:59,508
and became a piece of myth .

1338
01:06:59,599 --> 01:07:03,342
You could say that there 's lots of debates about intelligent design ,

1339
01:07:03,436 --> 01:07:06,599
and the creation myth versus the big bang ,

1340
01:07:06,689 --> 01:07:09,191
and other scientific explanations of the origin of the universe .

1341
01:07:09,275 --> 01:07:12,188
Well , I would argue that in the early 21st century ,

1342
01:07:12,654 --> 01:07:16,238
others have said , as well , that the real creation myth is Frankenstein .

1343
01:07:16,324 --> 01:07:19,942
In the age of bioengineering and tampering with biology

1344
01:07:20,036 --> 01:07:23,700
and genetic modification , the real creation myth is Frankenstein .

1345
01:07:23,790 --> 01:07:28,876
It 's the myth of a man-made universe rather than a God-made universe .

1346
01:07:28,962 --> 01:07:31,704
And one of the reasons why it is such a universal myth

1347
01:07:31,798 --> 01:07:34,961
is that this film carried it into all parts of the world .

1348
01:07:35,134 --> 01:07:37,216
It also Americanized it .

1349
01:07:37,470 --> 01:07:41,429
A British novel , turned into a British play in the 1820s ,

1350
01:07:41,516 --> 01:07:44,975
refracted through German expressionism in the 1920s ,

1351
01:07:45,061 --> 01:07:49,555
and turned into a piece of Americana in 1931 for Depression audiences .

1352
01:07:49,649 --> 01:07:51,640
[ CROWD CONTINUES SHOUTING ]

1353
01:07:51,734 --> 01:07:53,475
[ DOGS BARKING ]

1354
01:07:57,532 --> 01:08:00,240
- WOMAN : Have you got it ? - Yeah . Come on . Hurry .

1355
01:08:00,994 --> 01:08:02,200
Hurry up .

1356
01:08:02,287 --> 01:08:04,278
[ WHISPERING INDISTINCTLY ]

1357
01:08:07,375 --> 01:08:08,865
[ GIGGLING ]

1358
01:08:08,960 --> 01:08:12,129
FRAYLING : Here are the maids , giggling in the way that they were before .

1359
01:08:12,213 --> 01:08:15,751
An invention of James Whale for the fade-out ,

1360
01:08:15,842 --> 01:08:20,052
which is another reference to a son to the house of Frankenstein .

1361
01:08:20,179 --> 01:08:23,262
The great irony of the whole story of a motherless child ,

1362
01:08:23,349 --> 01:08:27,183
a piece of tampering with nature , versus a biological child .

1363
01:08:27,270 --> 01:08:30,433
You could just see that it isn 't Colin Clive lying on the bed .

1364
01:08:30,523 --> 01:08:32,434
He 'd gone home to London .

1365
01:08:34,569 --> 01:08:37,402
But this was such a relatively low-budget production

1366
01:08:37,488 --> 01:08:40,115
they couldn 't even get any end music specially written .

1367
01:08:40,199 --> 01:08:43,988
The music was written by Giuseppe Becce for silent films on the shelf ,

1368
01:08:44,078 --> 01:08:45,739
Grand Appassionato .

1369
01:08:45,830 --> 01:08:48,123
And that 's what they used behind the end credits .

1370
01:08:48,207 --> 01:08:52,292
Just as they used at the opening titles , Misterioso by Bernhard Kaun .

1371
01:08:52,378 --> 01:08:54,880
Later horror films would have special scores written for them .

1372
01:08:54,964 --> 01:08:56,750
This one really doesn 't .

1373
01:08:57,091 --> 01:09:01,210
And so ends a low-budget film that founded a genre ,

1374
01:09:01,304 --> 01:09:03,887
that created a corporate image for a studio

1375
01:09:03,973 --> 01:09:09,184
and that was imitated thousands of times , in all sorts of ways ,

1376
01:09:09,270 --> 01:09:11,386
for the balance of the 1930s .

1377
01:09:11,689 --> 01:09:15,102
Frankenstein had become a movie rather than a novel .

1378
01:09:15,193 --> 01:09:18,821
And on the end credits , Karloff is actually mentioned by name for the first time .

1379
01:09:18,905 --> 01:09:20,990
He 's a question mark on the opening credits

1380
01:09:21,074 --> 01:09:23,611
in a long tradition of not naming the monster .

1381
01:09:23,701 --> 01:09:26,443
But here he is , " The Monster , Boris Karloff . "

1382
01:09:26,537 --> 01:09:31,122
One , two , three ... Fourth down the cast list , and not even invited to the previews .

1383
01:09:31,209 --> 01:09:34,622
But it was the making of his career , and he was very grateful for it .

1384
01:09:34,712 --> 01:09:37,545
There were countless other Frankenstein monsters ,

1385
01:09:37,632 --> 01:09:41,091
but it was never to be so touching , so pathetic , again .

1386
01:09:41,177 --> 01:09:44,010
James Whale said the thing was to make it believable .

1387
01:09:44,097 --> 01:09:46,259
And he 'd certainly succeeded in that .

1388
01:09:46,766 --> 01:09:48,006
As Karloff wrote ,

1389
01:09:48,101 --> 01:09:50,394
" The most heartrending aspect of the creature 's life , "

1390
01:09:50,478 --> 01:09:53,766
" for us , was his ultimate desertion by his creator . "

1391
01:09:53,856 --> 01:09:56,223
And that had become the heart of the story .

1392
01:09:56,317 --> 01:09:58,433
[ INSTRUMENTAL MUSIC PLAYING ]

